==========================================================================
rvat.py -- RISC-V atomics, ordering and vectors, measured on the bytes
==========================================================================

  Every claim in this file carries one of three labels:

    MEASURED           about the compiler or the bytes, by experiment
    MEASURED-ON-BYTES  a property of emitted bytes, cross-checked against
                      llvm-objdump-21 as a second reader
    QUOTED             a manual claim, with a document and a section

  The two documents every QUOTED row names:
    rv32-unpriv  Volume I, the unprivileged architecture, v20260120
                 chapters 2, 12, 17, 18 and 30
    riscv-cc     the RISC-V ISA Calling Convention, v20230911

  NOTHING HERE IS EXECUTED.  There is no RISC-V machine, no emulator
  and no RISC-V linker on this host, so NO RESERVATION IS EVER HELD,
  NO AMO IS EVER OBSERVED TO BE ATOMIC, NO FENCE IS EVER OBSERVED TO
  ORDER ANYTHING, AND NO VECTOR INSTRUCTION IS EVER RUN.  There are
  NO TIMINGS and NO SPEEDUPS anywhere in this file.  THE
  SIBLINGS THAT MEASURED RATIOS MEASURED THEM ON HARDWARE AND THIS
  ONE HAS NO HARDWARE, SO THE RATIOS HAVE NO COUNTERPART HERE AND
  ARE NOT INVENTED TO FILL THE GAP.  Section 1 says so before the
  first measurement and section 14 says so after the last one.

  The decoder is BORROWED from courses/rvasm/assets/samples/
  (rvdec.py) and this file PREPENDS FOUR models of its own.
  `m_setivli` names vsetivli and vsetvl, which the inherited decoder
  cannot; `m_vector_mask` names the vector DATA path, which it
  declines; `m_amo_order` READS inst[26] and inst[25], which it never
  looks at; and `m_fence_sets` renders the fence sets as letters and
  names fence.tso, which it calls `fence`.  It edits no sibling;
  section 13's R1, R2 and R3 say what each one adds and why the
  sibling is untouched.

  Inherited decoder models: 26, of which this course PREPENDS 4.
  Total in the dispatch here: 30.

==========================================================================
SECTION 1 -- THE METHOD, AND WHAT IT MAY NOT CLAIM
==========================================================================

  There are FOUR ABSENCES on this host and this course's is a THIRD kind, which is worth being explicit about because the two RISC-V courses before it had different ones.

  * NO RISC-V MACHINE.  The host is x86-64.
  * NO EMULATOR.  qemu-riscv64 ABSENT, and spike ABSENT.
  * NO RISC-V LINKER.  `riscv64-linux-gnu-ld IS NOT INSTALLED` on this host, and
    neither are `riscv64-linux-gnu-gcc` or `riscv64-linux-gnu-as`, so there is
    not even a RISC-V LINKER to produce a running image.
  * NO SECOND ASSEMBLER.  GNU binutils has no RISC-V target here, so both
    readers of the two-reader check come from ONE LLVM TREE.

  NOTHING IN THIS COURSE IS EVER EXECUTED, AND NOT ONE INSTRUCTION IN IT HAS BEEN RUN. NO RESERVATION IS EVER HELD. NO STORE-CONDITIONAL IS EVER OBSERVED TO SUCCEED OR TO FAIL. NO AMO IS EVER OBSERVED
  TO BE ATOMIC. NO FENCE IS EVER OBSERVED TO ORDER ANYTHING. NO VECTOR INSTRUCTION IS EVER EXECUTED, so `vl` is never set by anything except a word in a file.

  AND THIS IS THE THIRD KIND OF ABSENCE IN THIS SECTION, AND THE THREE ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. The ABI course lost the ability to TIME. The privileged course lost the ability to OBSERVE THE SUBJECT
  and got a CSR address in its place. This one loses the ability to OBSERVE WHETHER ANY OF ITS INSTRUCTIONS DOES THE THING IT EXISTS TO DO -- because an atomic instruction is a PROMISE about
  observable behaviour and a fence is a RELATION between two sets of operations, and neither promise nor relation is a bit pattern.

  THE SIBLINGS THAT MEASURED TIMINGS ARE THE ONES THAT HAD A MACHINE, AND THEIR NUMBERS HAVE NO COUNTERPART HERE AND ARE NOT INVENTED TO FILL THE GAP. `simd` and `smp` taught vectors and ordering
  neutrally and measured speedups. `x86simd` measured a lost-update count on real hardware and found retraction 18 in it. `a64simd` measured 3.89x, 4.92x and 27.65x. THE HONEST ALTERNATIVE TO A RATIO
  IS NOT A SMALLER RATIO -- IT IS AN INSTRUCTION COUNT, A BYTE COUNT, A BIT POSITION, AND THE SENTENCE THAT A COUNT DOES NOT KNOW WHETHER THE INSTRUCTION IS FAST.

  WHAT SURVIVES IS EXACTLY THE PART A COMPILER AUTHOR NEEDS, and it is a lot: the atomic encoding and its two ordering bits, the compiler's decision about whether to emit an atomic at all, the four
  fields of `fence` and the fifteen spellings of its two sets, the four fields of `vsetvli` and the bit that is constant zero across all of them, the one-bit mask in three instruction groups, and what
  the compiler emits for a vectorised loop at four optimisation levels.

  Every claim below carries one of three labels and they are the section plan's rule 13: MEASURED, MEASURED-ON-BYTES, QUOTED. A label rendered two ways is not a label.


  THE TWO DOCUMENTS, and the short names every QUOTED row uses:

  rv32-unpriv RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume I: Unprivileged
  Architecture, v20260120 -- chapter 12 ("A", version 2.1), chapter 17
  (RVWMO), chapter 18 ("Ztso"), chapter 30 ("V") riscv-cc RISC-V ISA Calling
  Convention, v20230911

  And one thing this course does NOT do that its two siblings did: it does not
  shell out to a disassembler to obtain bytes. The second reader's LISTINGS
  are committed (`amo_objdump.txt`, `fence_objdump.txt`, `vec_objdump.txt`,
  `amo_a_O2_objdump.txt`, `amo_noa_O2_objdump.txt`, `fence_c_O2_objdump.txt`)
  so a reader with a hex editor and no cross-compiler can check every byte
  claim in the course. A course whose numbers are only checkable on the
  machine that wrote them is a course whose numbers are claims.


  THE CORPUS IS INCOMPLETE: no.  All 26 objects are present.
  Objects: 26.  Hand-written corpora: 3.  C corpora: 23.
  Decoder models inherited: 26, of which this course PREPENDS 4.
  Total in the dispatch here: 30.

==========================================================================
SECTION 2 -- THE ZERO -- NO IMPLICIT LOCK, AND WHAT IT COSTS TO SAY SO
==========================================================================

  THE MISSION DOCUMENT CALLS THIS THE "DIFFERS IN KIND" POINT, AND THE PLACE TO PROVE IT IS HERE, BEFORE ANY INSTRUCTION IS NAMED.

  x86-64 has a LOCK PREFIX. It is a property of the ENCODING, not of a separate instruction group: eighteen instructions accept it, the memory form of `xchg` is implicitly locked with or without it,
  and FORGETTING IT IS SILENT -- the instruction still assembles, still runs, and is simply not atomic. ON x86-64 THE ATOMICITY OF AN ORDINARY ARITHMETIC INSTRUCTION DEPENDS ON A PREFIX THAT IS NOT
  PART OF THE INSTRUCTION, AND A PROGRAMMER WHO OMITS IT GETS NO DIAGNOSTIC.

  RISC-V has no such thing. There is no prefix, there is no flag, and there is no implicit lock on any instruction. The atomics are a THIRD INSTRUCTION GROUP under their own opcode, and each one is
  atomic because of what it IS.

  And the honest way to state that is to count both sides, because "zero against eighteen" is a number a reader should be able to check.


  THE RISC-V SIDE.  The count of instructions in this corpus that
  take a lock prefix, measured by asking the assembler for one.

  assembled?  the instruction asked for        what came back                       
  0x00c58533  add a0, a1, a2                   an ordinary register-register add    
  0x00004188  lw a0, 0(a1)                     an ordinary load                     
  0x0000c188  sw a0, 0(a1)                     an ordinary store                    
  0x00b6252f  amoadd.w a0, a1, (a2)            an AMO, which is atomic anyway       
  0x1005a52f  lr.w a0, (a1)                    load-reserved, which is atomic anyway
  0x0330000f  fence rw, rw                     the ordering fence                   
  0x0d85f2d7  vsetvli t0, a1, e64, m1, ta, ma  a vector configuration               
  REFUSED     lock add a0, a1, a2              unexpected token                     
  REFUSED     lock lw a0, 0(a1)                unexpected token                     

  2 of the 9 candidates the assembler REFUSED, and every refusal
  involving the literal word `lock` is a refusal of the PREFIX
  ITSELF:

    `lock` is not a mnemonic, a modifier or a reserved word on
    this target.  It is a token the assembler does not know, and
    the diagnostic is `unexpected token`.

  THE COUNT: 0 instructions in the RISC-V corpus take a lock prefix.

  THE ZERO IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF FEATURES.  It is a DESIGN
  DECISION, and the way to tell the difference is to count the OTHER
  side: how many instructions ARE atomic with no prefix at all.  
  On x86-64 the count of "instructions that need a prefix to become
  atomic" is a list of ORDINARY ARITHMETIC and the prefix is a
  MODIFIER.  On RISC-V the atomics are a separate group under their
  own opcode and the prefix count is zero because there is nothing to
  modify -- and that is why a RISC-V binary can be disassembled by a
  tool that has never been told what atomicity is.

  THE x86-64 SIDE, MEASURED rather than quoted, because a contrast
  with an unmeasured number in it is a footnote.

  #   instruction  encoding
  1   addq                 
  2   adcq                 
  3   andq                 
  4   orq                  
  5   sbbq                 
  6   subq                 
  7   xorq                 
  8   cmpq                 
  9   testq                
  10  notq                 
  11  negq                 
  12  incl                 
  13  decl                 
  14  xaddq                
  15  cmpxchgq             
  16  xchgq                
  17  btsq                 
  18  btrq                 
  19  btcq                 
  20  cmpxchg8b            
  21  cmpxchg16b           
  22  btq                  

  22 lock-prefixed instructions assembled, and 0 unprefixed
  line in the file.  The DISTINCT mnemonics among them are 22:
    adc add and bt btc btr bts cmp cmpxchg cmpxchg16b cmpxchg8b decl incl neg not or sbb sub test xadd xchg xor

  AND THE ONE WITH NO LOCK IS IN THE LIST ABOVE ANYWAY,
  BECAUSE THE ASSEMBLER ACCEPTS `lock bt` -- and `bt` is
  not one of the SDM's eighteen, so what this file measures is
  what THIS ASSEMBLER ACCEPTS rather than what the architecture
  defines.  MEASURED, AND IT IS THE TRAP THIS
  WHOLE COURSE EXISTS TO NAME:


  THE STRUCTURAL POINT, and it is the one a reader should carry out of the page: the reason x86-64's list is eighteen ORDINARY instructions and RISC-V's is thirteen ATOMIC ones is not a difference of
  list length. It is that x86-64 made atomicity a PROPERTY OF AN EXISTING INSTRUCTION and RISC-V made it a CATEGORY OF INSTRUCTION. A PROPERTY AND A CATEGORY ARE DIFFERENT OBJECTS, AND THE DIFFERENCE
  IS VISIBLE IN WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU FORGET: on x86-64 forgetting is silent, and on RISC-V forgetting is not possible, because there is nothing to forget -- the ordinary add is not atomic and never
  claims to be, and the atomic is a different instruction with a different name that a reader can see in a disassembly and check in a review.


  AND THE COST OF THE DESIGN IS WORTH NAMING, because a course that only advertises is not a reference. PUTTING THE ATOMICS IN THEIR OWN GROUP COSTS THE PROGRAMMER something specific, and the honest
  name for it is that it costs the programmer the ordinary-operator shortcut, so the replacement is a retry loop that has to be written by hand. Compare-exchange is two words of x86 assembly with a
  `lock` prefix; on RISC-V it is a `lr`/`sc` pair inside a loop, because there is no `cmpxchg` in the base A extension at all. A DESIGN THAT REMOVES A SILENT FAILURE ALSO REMOVES A SHORTCUT, AND THE
  HONEST FORM OF THE TRADE IS TO NAME BOTH SIDES RATHER THAN TO COUNT THE FEATURES. Section 5 measures the loop the compiler emits for it and section 6 measures the case where it emits a library call
  instead, and the whole of the difference between those two sections is ONE LETTER in a command line.


==========================================================================
SECTION 3 -- THE ELEVEN OPERATIONS, AND THE ONE BIT THAT IS THE WIDTH
==========================================================================

  MEASURED-ON-BYTES. Everything in this section is read out of a real object a real assembler produced, and the second reader confirms the words.

  The A extension is ONE opcode, 0101111, and inside it a FIVE-BIT funct5 at inst[31:27]. Eleven of the thirty-two funct5 values are named. The next two bits, inst[26] and inst[25], are the ordering,
  and section 4 reads them. Then the three register fields, then a THREE-BIT funct3 of which only TWO values are AMO widths -- 010 is a word and 011 is a doubleword.

  AND HERE IS THE FIRST REAL FINDING, which is that the width is ONE BIT. `amoadd.w` and `amoadd.d` are the same instruction with the same operands and they differ by exactly 0x1000, which is bit 12
  -- the LOW bit of funct3. THE `.w` AND `.d` SUFFIXES LOOK LIKE A TYPE AND THEY ARE A SINGLE BIT, AND A FIELD WITH TWO NAMES IS NOT A TYPE, IT IS A BIT WITH TWO SPELLINGS. Compare `fadd.s` against
  `fadd.d` in the sibling AArch64 material, which is also one bit -- and the reason it is worth knowing is that a bit with two names is a bit a decoder can read with one table.


  THE ELEVEN OPERATIONS AT BOTH WIDTHS, read out of amo.o and paired
  with `amo.s` BY POSITION -- which is CHECKED, not assumed.

  operation  word        assembled  funct5  funct3  aq  rl  bytes
  amoadd     0x00b6252f  amoadd.w   00000   2       0   0   4    
  amoadd     0x00b6352f  amoadd.d   00000   3       0   0   4    
  amoswap    0x08b6252f  amoswap.w  00001   2       0   0   4    
  amoswap    0x08b6352f  amoswap.d  00001   3       0   0   4    
  amoxor     0x20b6252f  amoxor.w   00100   2       0   0   4    
  amoxor     0x20b6352f  amoxor.d   00100   3       0   0   4    
  amoand     0x60b6252f  amoand.w   01100   2       0   0   4    
  amoand     0x60b6352f  amoand.d   01100   3       0   0   4    
  amoor      0x40b6252f  amoor.w    01000   2       0   0   4    
  amoor      0x40b6352f  amoor.d    01000   3       0   0   4    
  amomin     0x80b6252f  amomin.w   10000   2       0   0   4    
  amomin     0x80b6352f  amomin.d   10000   3       0   0   4    
  amomax     0xa0b6252f  amomax.w   10100   2       0   0   4    
  amomax     0xa0b6352f  amomax.d   10100   3       0   0   4    
  amominu    0xc0b6252f  amominu.w  11000   2       0   0   4    
  amominu    0xc0b6352f  amominu.d  11000   3       0   0   4    
  amomaxu    0xe0b6252f  amomaxu.w  11100   2       0   0   4    
  amomaxu    0xe0b6352f  amomaxu.d  11100   3       0   0   4    
  lr         0x1005a52f  lr.w       00010   2       0   0   4    
  lr         0x1005b52f  lr.d       00010   3       0   0   4    
  sc         0x18b6252f  sc.w       00011   2       0   0   4    
  sc         0x18b6352f  sc.d       00011   3       0   0   4    
  amoswap    0x0805a52f  amoswap.w  00001   2       0   0   4    
  amoswap    0x0805b52f  amoswap.d  00001   3       0   0   4    
  amoadd     0x00b6202f  amoadd.w   00000   2       0   0   4    
  amoor      0x40b6252f  amoor.w    01000   2       0   0   4    

  11 distinct operations, 11 distinct funct5 values, 2 widths,
  4 bytes for every one of them, which is the arithmetic of an R
  format with three register fields and nothing else.

  THE WIDTH IS ONE BIT.  Every `.w`/`.d` pair in the corpus, XORed:

  the pair               XOR         bits that moved  reading         
  amoadd.w / amoadd.d    0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amoswap.w / amoswap.d  0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amoxor.w / amoxor.d    0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amoand.w / amoand.d    0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amoor.w / amoor.d      0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amomin.w / amomin.d    0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amomax.w / amomax.d    0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amominu.w / amominu.d  0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amomaxu.w / amomaxu.d  0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  lr.w / lr.d            0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  sc.w / sc.d            0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3
  amoswap.w / amoswap.d  0x00001000  1                bit 12 of funct3

  12 of 12 pairs XOR to exactly 0x1000.

  A SUFFIX IS NOT A TYPE.  On x86-64 the LOCK is a prefix and
  the width is a suffix, and the two are DIFFERENT MECHANISMS; on
  RISC-V the ordering is two bits INSIDE the word and the width is
  one more, and both of them are ordinary fields of an ordinary
  encoding.  THAT IS WHY A RISC-V ATOMIC INSTRUCTION IS FOUR
  BYTES AND AN x86-64 LOCKED ONE IS ONE TO FOUR PLUS A PREFIX: THE
  PREFIX IS AN EXTRA BYTE THE DECODER HAS TO RECOGNISE BEFORE IT
  CAN KNOW THE LENGTH.

  THE FIVE-BIT FIELD HAS ELEVEN NAMES AND TWENTY-ONE HOLES, and the
  holes are printed rather than omitted, because a table with a gap
  and a table with a hole are different tables.

  0b00000 = 0      amoadd                             
  0b00001 = 1      amoswap                            
  0b00010 = 2      lr                                 lr
  0b00011 = 3      sc                                 sc
  0b00100 = 4      amoxor                             
  0b00101 = 5      (no name in the A extension)       
  0b00110 = 6      (no name in the A extension)       
  0b00111 = 7      (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01000 = 8      amoor                              
  0b01001 = 9      (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01010 = 10     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01011 = 11     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01100 = 12     amoand                             
  0b01101 = 13     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01110 = 14     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b01111 = 15     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10000 = 16     amomin                             
  0b10001 = 17     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10010 = 18     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10011 = 19     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10100 = 20     amomax                             
  0b10101 = 21     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10110 = 22     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b10111 = 23     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11000 = 24     amominu                            
  0b11001 = 25     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11010 = 26     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11011 = 27     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11100 = 28     amomaxu                            
  0b11101 = 29     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11110 = 30     (no name in the A extension)       
  0b11111 = 31     (no name in the A extension)       

  11 named of 32, so 21 unnamed, and 2 of the eleven are the
  reservation pair -- which is why the A extension is not "eleven
  atomics" but "nine atomics and a two-instruction construct that no
  single instruction is".

  THE ELEVEN funct5 VALUES ARE NOT EVENLY SPACED, and the spacing is
  a history rather than an accident of a field map:

    0b00000 -> 0b00001   gap 1
    0b00001 -> 0b00010   gap 1
    0b00010 -> 0b00011   gap 1
    0b00011 -> 0b00100   gap 1
    0b00100 -> 0b01000   gap 4
    0b01000 -> 0b01100   gap 4
    0b01100 -> 0b10000   gap 4
    0b10000 -> 0b10100   gap 4
    0b10100 -> 0b11000   gap 4
    0b11000 -> 0b11100   gap 4

  AND THE FOUR BITWISE OPERATIONS ARE 0, 4, 8 and 12 -- a stride of
  FOUR -- while the four ORDERING comparisons are 16, 20, 24 and 28.
  THE BITS ARE GROUPED IN FOURS, WHICH IS A SIGN THAT THE
  SIGNED AND UNSIGNED HALVES SHARE A SLOT AND THE ENCODER GAVE EACH
  HALF A NIBBLE, AND A READER WHO EXPECTS ELEVEN EVENLY SPACED VALUES
  WILL WRITE A DECODER WITH ELEVEN CASES WHERE THREE WOULD DO.

  And the reservations sit at 2 and 3, INSIDE the stride-4 block,
  between the arithmetic pair and the bitwise block.  THE TWO
  INSTRUCTIONS THAT ARE NOT ATOMIC OPERATIONS ARE THE TWO THAT
  IMPLEMENT A CONSTRUCT, AND THE ENCODER PUT THEM WHERE A CONSTRUCT
  BELONGS: next to each other, at 2 and 3, in the first sixteen.

==========================================================================
SECTION 4 -- aq AND rl ARE TWO ADJACENT BITS, AND THE XOR PROVES IT
==========================================================================

  MEASURED-ON-BYTES, and this is the section's cleanest single result.

  The A extension spends FIVE bits at inst[31:27] on WHICH OPERATION to
  perform and TWO MORE at inst[26] and inst[25] on how it is ordered. The bits
  are adjacent. `aq` is inst[26] and `rl` is inst[25], and there is no shift
  and no scrambling between them and their positions in the mnemonic -- which
  is the opposite of the immediates in the base ISA, where the S format splits
  one twelve-bit immediate across inst[31:25] and inst[11:7].

  The measurement is an XOR. Take an instruction, set `aq`, subtract -- and
  the answer is a single bit. Do it at both widths, on all three of
  load-reserved, store-conditional and the AMOs, and every single XOR is the
  same two numbers.


  EVERY aq XOR IN THE CORPUS.  Each row is one instruction, the same
  instruction with `.aq`, and the two words side by side.

  with            with it / without      XOR         bits  which bits    
  amoadd.w.aq     0x04b6252f/0x00b6252f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  amoadd.w.rl     0x02b6252f/0x00b6252f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  amoadd.w.aqrl   0x06b6252f/0x00b6252f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  amoadd.d.aq     0x04b6352f/0x00b6352f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  amoadd.d.rl     0x02b6352f/0x00b6352f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  amoadd.d.aqrl   0x06b6352f/0x00b6352f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  amoswap.w.aq    0x0cb6252f/0x08b6252f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  amoswap.w.rl    0x0ab6252f/0x08b6252f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  amoswap.w.aqrl  0x0eb6252f/0x08b6252f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  lr.w.aq         0x1405a52f/0x1005a52f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  lr.w.rl         0x1205a52f/0x1005a52f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  lr.w.aqrl       0x1605a52f/0x1005a52f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  lr.d.aq         0x1405b52f/0x1005b52f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  lr.d.aqrl       0x1605b52f/0x1005b52f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  sc.w.aq         0x1cb6252f/0x18b6252f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  sc.w.rl         0x1ab6252f/0x18b6252f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  sc.w.aqrl       0x1eb6252f/0x18b6252f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26
  sc.d.aq         0x1cb6352f/0x18b6352f  0x04000000  1     bit 26        
  sc.d.rl         0x1ab6352f/0x18b6352f  0x02000000  1     bit 25        
  sc.d.aqrl       0x1eb6352f/0x18b6352f  0x06000000  2     bit 25, bit 26

  THE SET OF aq XORs OVER THE WHOLE CORPUS: 0x04000000
  THE SET OF rl XORs OVER THE WHOLE CORPUS: 0x02000000
  THE SET OF aqrl XORs OVER THE WHOLE CORPUS: 0x06000000

  ONE NUMBER FOR aq AND ONE FOR rl, ACROSS EVERY OPERATION, AT
  EVERY WIDTH, ON ALL THREE KINDS OF INSTRUCTION.  THE aq BIT
  IS BIT 26 AND THE rl BIT IS BIT 25, AND THEY ARE ADJACENT, AND
  THAT IS THE WHOLE ENCODING.  IT IS NOT A PREFIX, IT IS NOT A
  SECOND INSTRUCTION, AND IT COSTS TWO OF THE THIRTY-TWO BITS OF A
  FOUR-BYTE WORD.

  AND THE THREE ARE ADDITIVE, which is the cheapest possible proof
  that they are TWO FIELDS and not one field with three values:

  instruction             word        XOR with the plain form  bits moved
  amoadd.w a0, a1, (a2)   0x00b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amoadd.d a0, a1, (a2)   0x00b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amoswap.w a0, a1, (a2)  0x08b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amoswap.d a0, a1, (a2)  0x08b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amoxor.w a0, a1, (a2)   0x20b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amoxor.d a0, a1, (a2)   0x20b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amoand.w a0, a1, (a2)   0x60b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amoand.d a0, a1, (a2)   0x60b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amoor.w a0, a1, (a2)    0x40b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amoor.d a0, a1, (a2)    0x40b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amomin.w a0, a1, (a2)   0x80b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amomin.d a0, a1, (a2)   0x80b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amomax.w a0, a1, (a2)   0xa0b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amomax.d a0, a1, (a2)   0xa0b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amominu.w a0, a1, (a2)  0xc0b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amominu.d a0, a1, (a2)  0xc0b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amomaxu.w a0, a1, (a2)  0xe0b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  amomaxu.d a0, a1, (a2)  0xe0b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  lr.w a0, (a1)           0x1005a52f  0x00000000               0         
  lr.d a0, (a1)           0x1005b52f  0x00000000               0         
  sc.w a0, a1, (a2)       0x18b6252f  0x00000000               0         
  sc.d a0, a1, (a2)       0x18b6352f  0x00000000               0         
  amoswap.w a0, x0, (a1)  0x0805a52f  0x00000000               0         
  amoswap.d a0, x0, (a1)  0x0805b52f  0x00000000               0         
  amoadd.w x0, a1, (a2)   0x00b6202f  0x00000000               0         
  amoor.w a0, a1, (a2)    0x40b6252f  0x00000000               0         

  THE XORs ARE ADDITIVE -- aq|rl is the bitwise OR of the two
  single-bit XORs and NOT A THIRD BIT -- AND ADDITIVITY IS WHAT MAKES
  THEM FIELDS.  A DECODER CAN RECOVER A TWO-BIT FIELD BY OR-ing
  TWO SINGLE-BIT MASKS, AND CANNOT RECOVER A SINGLE FIELD WITH FOUR
  VALUES THAT WAY, SO THE MEASUREMENT IS NOT JUST A CONFIRMATION OF
  THE POSITIONS, IT IS THE PROOF THAT THESE ARE TWO FIELDS.

  WHAT THE TWO BITS ARE FOR is QUOTED, and the quotation is the
  point rather than the padding:

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 12, section 12.1.1:
      "If only the aq bit is set, the atomic memory operation is
       treated as an acquire access...  If only the rl bit is set,
       the atomic memory operation is treated as a release access.
       If both the aq and rl bits are set, the atomic memory
       operation is sequentially consistent..."

  SO THE FOUR SPELLINGS ARE FOUR SEMANTICS AND THE ENCODING
  SPENDS TWO BITS ON ALL FOUR.  Compare the other two architectures
  in this collection, where the SAME four semantics are reachable
  but by DIFFERENT MECHANISMS: x86-64 spells sequentially consistent
  as an unprefixed `xchg` and puts no bits in the instruction at all,
  and AArch64 spells acquire and release as ACCESS MODES on the
  instruction itself -- `ldar` and `stlr` are different opcodes from
  `ldr` and `str` -- and spells sequentially consistent as a
  COMBINATION of the two.  RISC-V SPENDS TWO BITS WHERE AARCH64
  SPENDS TWO OPCODES AND x86-64 SPENDS NOTHING, AND THE REASON IS THAT
  RISC-V DID NOT WANT A FOURTH INSTRUCTION GROUP.  THE THREE
  COURSES SPEND DIFFERENT THINGS ON THE SAME FOUR SEMANTICS, AND A
  THIRD OF THE QUESTION IS WHICH OF THE THREE IS CHEAPEST TO DECODE.

  AND THE SAME FOUR SEMANTICS ARE REACHABLE WITH A FENCE INSTEAD,
  which is the thing that makes the RISC-V model different IN KIND
  rather than in degree.  The manual says it outright:

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 12, section 12.1.4:
      "Although the FENCE R, RW instruction suffices to implement
       the acquire operation and FENCE RW, W suffices to implement
       release, both imply additional unnecessary ordering as
       compared to AMOs with the corresponding aq or rl bit set."

  SO A RISC-V ATOMIC IS A FENCE WITH THE UNNECESSARY PART
  REMOVED, AND THE TWO BITS ARE WHERE THE REMOVAL IS RECORDED.  
  THAT IS WHY `lr.w.aqrl` AND `fence r, rw` ARE NOT TWO WAYS OF
  SAYING THE SAME THING, AND WHY THE COURSE HAS TO MEASURE BOTH
  SIDES RATHER THAN CHOOSING ONE: a reader who only ever reads the
  fence form will not notice that the bits exist, and a reader who
  only ever reads the bits will not know what relation between two
  sets of operations the fence form is expressing.

  MEASURED, to close the loop: the compiler emits BOTH.  Section 6
  counts the `fence` instructions in fence_c_O2.o and section 5
  counts the `.aq`/`.rl` suffixes in amo_a_O2.o, and neither count is
  zero.

==========================================================================
SECTION 5 -- lr AND sc -- WHY THE RETRY IS THE INSTRUCTION
==========================================================================

  MEASURED-ON-BYTES for the encodings, QUOTED for the reservation rules, and the division is not a convenience -- it is the boundary of the whole concept.

  x86-64 has a compare-and-swap. `cmpxchg` with a `lock` prefix is ONE instruction and it either swaps or does not, and a loop around it is a performance habit rather than a requirement. RISC-V HAS NO
  COMPARE-AND-SWAP IN THE BASE A EXTENSION AT ALL. WHAT IT HAS IS TWO INSTRUCTIONS THAT MUST BE USED IN PAIRS AND THAT REPORT FAILURE IN A REGISTER, AND A PAIR THAT REPORTS FAILURE TO A CALLER WHO
  MUST BRANCH IS NOT A PAIR, IT IS A LOOP.

  So the retry is not syntax around the instruction. THE RETRY IS NOT SYNTAX AROUND THE INSTRUCTION -- IT IS WHAT THE INSTRUCTION IS, AND THERE IS NO ONE-INSTRUCTION FORM OF COMPARE-AND-SWAP FOR IT TO
  BE SYNTAX AROUND.


  THE TWO HALVES, as the assembler spells them and as the bits read
  them.  `lr` is funct5 = 0b00010 with rs2 FORCED TO ZERO; `sc` is
  funct5 = 0b00011 with a real rs2.

  assembled  word        funct5  rs2  rs1  rd  funct3
  lr.w       0x1005a52f  00010   0    11   10  2     
  lr.d       0x1005b52f  00010   0    11   10  3     
  sc.w       0x18b6252f  00011   11   12   10  2     
  sc.d       0x18b6352f  00011   11   12   10  3     

  rs2 = 0 ON EVERY lr AND rs2 = 11 ON EVERY sc, AND THAT IS
  NOT A CONVENTION: A DECODER THAT DOES NOT CHECK IT WILL NAME
  `lr.w` WITH A NON-ZERO rs2, WHICH IS A WORD THE ARCHITECTURE DOES
  NOT DEFINE.  AN rs2 FIELD THAT IS ALWAYS ZERO IN ONE INSTRUCTION
  AND ALWAYS MEANINGFUL IN THE NEXT IS NOT AN UNUSED FIELD, IT IS A
  CONSTRAINT THE ENCODER ENFORCES AND THE DECODER MUST CHECK.

  AND THE ASSEMBLER ENFORCES IT, which is a measurement and not a
  quotation:

  the instruction asked for  result   what it is testing                 the assembler's own words                     
  lr.w a0, a1, (a2)          REFUSED  lr with a THIRD operand            expected '(' or optional integer offset       
  lr.d a0, a1, (a2)          REFUSED  the same at the other width        expected '(' or optional integer offset       
  sc.w a0, (a1)              REFUSED  sc with only TWO operands          invalid operand for instruction               
  amoadd.w a0, (a1)          REFUSED  an AMO with only TWO operands      invalid operand for instruction               
  lr.w.t a0, (a1)            REFUSED  a MASKED lr, which does not exist  unrecognized instruction mnemonic, did you mea
  sc.w.t a0, a1, (a2)        REFUSED  a MASKED sc, which does not exist  unrecognized instruction mnemonic, did you mea
  amoadd.b a0, a1, (a2)      REFUSED  a BYTE AMO, which is Zabha         instruction requires the following: 'Zabha' (B

  `lr.w` WITH A THIRD OPERAND IS REFUSED WITH "EXPECTED '(' OR
  OPTIONAL INTEGER OFFSET" -- WHICH IS THE ASSEMBLER SAYING THE
  INSTRUCTION HAS NO rs2 TO GIVE, NOT THAT THE SYNTAX IS WRONG.  
  A REFUSAL IS THE CHEAPEST PROOF THAT A CONSTRAINT IS REAL, AND THIS
  ONE IS ALSO A REMINDER THAT A DIAGNOSTIC ABOUT SPELLING CAN BE
  A DIAGNOSTIC ABOUT SEMANTICS.

  AND `amoadd.b` IS REFUSED WITH "INSTRUCTION REQUIRES THE FOLLOWING:
  'Zabha'" -- so the byte-width AMO is not missing from RISC-V, it
  is in a DIFFERENT EXTENSION, and the assembler names the extension
  rather than saying the instruction does not exist.  THE
  DIAGNOSTIC NAMES THE LETTER THAT WOULD MAKE IT WORK, WHICH IS A
  BETTER ERROR THAN "UNKNOWN INSTRUCTION" AND A REMINDER THAT THE
  ISA IS A SET OF DOCUMENTS.

  THE LOOP, MEASURED.  `amo_cas_loop` in amo.c is a hand-written
  `do { } while` around a C11 compare-exchange, and this is what
  clang 21.1.8 emits for it at -march=rv64ima -O2.

  word        mnemonic   operands                     bytes
  0x0005869b  sext.w     a3, a1                       4    
  0x160525af  lr.w.aqrl  a1, (a0)                     4    
  0xfed59ce3  bne        a1, a3, 0x40 <amo_cas_loop>  4    
  0x1ac5272f  sc.w.rl    a4, a2, (a0)                 4    
  0xfe071ae3  bnez       a4, 0x44 <amo_cas_loop+0x4>  4    
  0x00068513  mv         a0, a3                       4    
  0x00008067  ret                                     4    

  7 instructions: 1 lr, 1 sc, 2 branches, and the rest is the
  comparison of the loaded value against the expected one.

  THE LOOP IS IN THE OUTPUT.  There is no library call and no
  function boundary: the four instructions that implement one attempt
  are the four instructions the source asked for, and the BRANCH
  AFTER THE `sc` IS THE RETRY.  THE COMPILER DID NOT CHOOSE A
  COMPARE-AND-SWAP BECAUSE THERE IS NOT ONE TO CHOOSE.  THERE IS NO
  `cmpxchg` IN THE BASE A EXTENSION, SO THE ONLY WAY TO EXPRESS
  COMPARE-AND-SWAP IS TO WRITE THE LOOP -- AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT
  KIND OF DIFFERENCE FROM x86-64, WHERE THE COMPILER HAS A
  COMPARE-AND-SWAP AND CHOOSES ACCORDING TO COST.

  AND THE `.aqrl` ON THE lr AND THE `.rl` ON THE sc ARE NOT A
  TYPO.  MEASURED: the two halves of a sequentially consistent pair
  do NOT both get both bits, and that asymmetry is the whole of the
  model --

    0x160525af  lr.w.aqrl    aq=1 rl=1
    0x1ac5272f  sc.w.rl      aq=0 rl=1

  THE ACQUIRE BIT GOES ON THE LOAD AND THE RELEASE BIT GOES ON
  THE STORE, AND NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND, AND THE MANUAL SAYS WHY:

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 12, section 12.1.2:
      "...software should not set the rl bit on an LR instruction
       unless the aq bit is also set, nor should software set the aq
       bit on an SC instruction unless the rl bit is also set.  LR.rl
       and SC.aq..."

  So `lr.w.aqrl` and `sc.w.rl` is the CORRECT spelling of a
  sequentially consistent pair and the compiler got it right, and
  the reason it matters is that the two-bit field admits four
  combinations and only three of the four are meaningful on each
  instruction.  A TWO-BIT FIELD WITH FOUR VALUES AND THREE OF
  THEM VALID ON HALF THE INSTRUCTIONS IS A FIELD WHOSE VALIDITY
  DEPENDS ON A NEIGHBOUR FIELD, AND A DECODER THAT READS IT WITHOUT
  READING WHICH INSTRUCTION IT IS ON WILL NAME FOUR THINGS THAT
  ARE TWO.

  THE `__sync` SPELLING, for contrast, because it costs the compiler
  MORE and the reason is a signature difference rather than a
  hardware difference:

  word        mnemonic   operands                      bytes
  0x160526af  lr.w.aqrl  a3, (a0)                      4    
  0xfeb69ee3  bne        a3, a1, 0x5c <sync_cas_loop>  4    
  0x1ac5272f  sc.w.rl    a4, a2, (a0)                  4    
  0xfe071ae3  bnez       a4, 0x5c <sync_cas_loop>      4    
  0x00100513  li         a0, 0x1                       4    
  0x00008067  ret                                      4    

  6 instructions, 1 lr and 1 sc -- so 1 ATTEMPTS are spelled out
  where the __atomic spelling needed one.  THE REASON IS THAT
  __sync_bool_compare_and_swap TAKES NO EXPECTED-OUT POINTER: the
  compiler cannot learn the memory word's value from a failed
  attempt, so it RELOADS it and compares again.  SAME
  HARDWARE, SAME INSTRUCTION PAIR, TWO INSTRUCTIONS PER ATTEMPT
  AGAINST ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS ENTIRELY IN THE CALLING
  CONVENTION OF THE SOURCE LANGUAGE.

  THE RESERVATION ITSELF IS QUOTED, and this is the honest boundary
  of the whole concept:

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 12, section 12.1.2, on what SC must do:
      "The SC must fail if the address is not within the reservation
       set of the most recent LR in program order.  The SC must fail
       if a store to the reservation set from another hart can be
       observed to occur between the LR and SC."

    and on what may sit between them:
      "The invalidation of a hart's reservation when it executes an
       LR or SC imply that a hart can only hold ONE RESERVATION AT A
       TIME, and that an SC can only pair with the most recent LR,"
       and LR with the next following SC, in program order."

    and on the guarantee that makes the loop TERMINATE rather than spin,
    which is the question a reader of a retry loop asks next.  The
    manual's eventuality guarantee (rv32-unpriv, chapter 12, section
    12.1.3) is PARAPHRASED here rather than quoted, because the
    sentence is long and the part that matters is short: a CONSTRAINED
    sequence of LR/SC pairs -- constrained to the pair, with nothing
    else between the two halves -- must eventually make progress.

  AND THAT LAST CLAUSE IS THE WHOLE CALLING-CONVENTION ARGUMENT,
  BECAUSE THE CONSTRAINT IS WHAT THE CONVENTION HAS TO PROTECT: a call,
  a second `lr`, or anything else between the halves is what turns a
  GUARANTEED-TO-PROGRESS LOOP INTO AN UNBOUNDED ONE.  THE PROGRESS
  GUARANTEE IS QUOTED HERE AND NOT MEASURED, BECAUSE IT IS A STATEMENT
  ABOUT WHAT AN IMPLEMENTATION MUST DO, AND NO IMPLEMENTATION ON THIS
  HOST HAS BEEN ASKED.
  ONE RESERVATION PER HART. THAT IS THE RULE THAT MAKES THE LOOP COST SOMETHING AND IT IS NOT IN THE ENCODING: `lr` does not name a reservation and `sc` does not name one either, and two `lr`
  instructions in one function INVALIDATE EACH OTHER, so the compiler must not keep a value in a register across an `lr` -- because keeping it in a register is not what invalidates it, but CALLING A
  FUNCTION is, and a function call in the middle of a pair kills the reservation.

  AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT CONSTRAINT FROM x86-64'S, WHERE THE LOCK IS HELD FOR THE DURATION OF ONE INSTRUCTION AND A SEQUENCE LOCK NEEDS AN EXPLICIT DANCE. HERE THE RESERVATION SPANS INSTRUCTIONS, SO
  ANYTHING THAT CAN INTERRUPT OR CALL IS A HAZARD, AND THE MANUAL SAYS THE MITIGATION OUT LOUD: "A store-conditional instruction to a SCRATCH WORD of memory should be used to forcibly invalidate any
  existing load reservation: during a preemptive context switch, and if necessary when changing virtual to physical address mappings."

  MEASURED, AND THIS IS THE LINE THE COURSE WILL NOT CROSS: none of the rules above has been OBSERVED, because no reservation is ever held on this host. There is no hart here to hold one, no other
  hart to invalidate it, and no scheduler to preempt the thread between the two halves. What is measured is that clang emits an `lr.w.aqrl` and an `sc.w.rl` with a branch between them, which is a fact
  about a compiler and not a fact about an atomic.


==========================================================================
SECTION 6 -- THE COMPILER'S DECISION -- ONE LETTER, TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS
==========================================================================

  MEASURED, and it is the course's central experiment because it needs no timing at all.

  The same C11 source, compiled twice. The ONLY difference is the letter `a` in -march. AND THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE IN THE OUTPUT IS VISIBLE IN A DISASSEMBLY, WITH NO EXECUTION AND NO CYCLE COUNT
  ANYWHERE.

  This is the measurement that makes "atomics are the compiler's choice" mean something. A reader can see, without a benchmark, that with the extension the compiler INLINES a four-instruction retry
  loop and without it the compiler CALLS A LIBRARY FUNCTION -- and that the library function is named `__atomic_compare_exchange_4`, which is itself the clearest possible statement of the width story:
  the name has a 4 in it because the width is a template parameter in the fallback path and a bit in the hardware path.

  THAT IS THE SAME WIDTH STORY AS SECTION 3 ARRIVING FROM THE OTHER DIRECTION. ONE BIT AT inst[12] WHEN THE EXTENSION IS PRESENT, AND A TEMPLATE PARAMETER IN A SYMBOL NAME WHEN IT IS NOT.


  WITH the A extension  (-march=rv64ima)

  function               instr  lr  sc  amo*  call  fence
  amo_cas_loop           7      1   1   0     0     0    
  amo_exchange_relaxed   2      0   0   1     0     0    
  amo_exchange_seqcst    2      0   0   1     0     0    
  amo_fetch_add_relaxed  2      0   0   1     0     0    
  amo_fetch_add_seqcst   2      0   0   1     0     0    
  amo_load_acquire       3      0   0   0     0     1    
  amo_or_relaxed         2      0   0   1     0     0    
  amo_store_release      3      0   0   0     0     1    
  sync_cas_loop          6      1   1   0     0     0    

    TOTAL 29 instructions: 2 lr, 2 sc, 5 amo*, 0 CALLS.

  WITHOUT it          (-march=rv64im) 

  function               instr  lr  sc  amo*  call  fence
  amo_cas_loop           25     0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_exchange_relaxed   9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_exchange_seqcst    9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_fetch_add_relaxed  9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_fetch_add_seqcst   9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_load_acquire       9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_or_relaxed         9      0   0   0     1     0    
  amo_store_release      8      0   0   0     1     0    
  sync_cas_loop          24     0   0   0     1     0    

    TOTAL 111 instructions: 0 lr, 0 sc, 0 amo*, 9 CALLS.

  THE CALL COLUMN IS COUNTED FROM THE RELOCATION TABLE, NOT FROM A
  MNEMONIC, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS: a call in a RELOCATABLE RISC-V
  OBJECT IS AN `auipc`/`jalr` PAIR WHOSE TARGET IS NAMED IN A
  RELOCATION, so there is no `call` pseudo-instruction to count and the
  first version of this table printed ZERO CALLS FOR BOTH BUILDS while
  the sentence under it said every atomic without the letter is a call.
  A TABLE THAT SAYS ZERO BESIDE A SENTENCE SAYING "EVERY ONE" IS A
  TABLE THAT HAS MEASURED THE WRONG INSTRUCTION, AND IT IS THE MOST
  DANGEROUS KIND OF WRONG BECAUSE THE SENTENCE IS CORRECT.

  AND THE CALLERS ARE NAMED, because the width is in the NAME and that
  is the same width story the encoding tells:

    __atomic_compare_exchange_4
    __atomic_exchange_4
    __atomic_fetch_add_4
    __atomic_fetch_or_4
    __atomic_load_4
    __atomic_store_4

  THE SAME TABLE, AS A DIFFERENCE, because the interesting number is
  not either column:

  function               instr with -> without  delta  atomic instr with  atomic instr OR call without  inlined only with a
  amo_cas_loop           7 -> 25                +18    2                  1                                                
  amo_exchange_relaxed   2 -> 9                 +7     1                  1                                                
  amo_exchange_seqcst    2 -> 9                 +7     1                  1                                                
  amo_fetch_add_relaxed  2 -> 9                 +7     1                  1                                                
  amo_fetch_add_seqcst   2 -> 9                 +7     1                  1                                                
  amo_load_acquire       3 -> 9                 +6     0                  1                             yes                
  amo_or_relaxed         2 -> 9                 +7     1                  1                                                
  amo_store_release      3 -> 8                 +5     0                  1                             yes                
  sync_cas_loop          6 -> 24                +18    2                  1                                                

  MEASURED: without the letter, every C11 atomic in the file is
  a CALL; with it, none is.  THE COMPILER DOES NOT "PREFER" ONE
  FORM.  IT HAS ONE FORM AND THE FORM IS A FUNCTION OF THE -march
  STRING.  THAT IS THE PAYLOAD OF THE WHOLE A EXTENSION AS A
  COMPILER INTERFACE: THE INSTRUCTION IS NOT A BETTER WAY TO DO IT,
  IT IS THE ONLY WAY TO DO IT WITHOUT A CALL, AND WHETHER THE CALL
  HAPPENS IS DECIDED BEFORE THE COMPILER LOOKS AT YOUR CODE.

  AND THE ROW THAT IS WORTH READING TWICE IS `amo_exchange_seqcst`,
  which is ONE instruction when the extension is there:

    0x0eb5252f  amoswap.w.aqrl     aq=1 rl=1
    0x00008067  ret                aq=0 rl=0

  `amoswap.w.aqrl` -- FOUR BYTES, no prefix, no branch, no loop.
  AND THE HARDCODED CLAIM THIS COURSE WAS WRITTEN AGAINST WAS
  THAT THE COMPILER WOULD EMIT `amoswap.w` "INSTEAD OF A HAND-ROLLED
  COMPARE-EXCHANGE".  IT DOES NOT, BECAUSE THE HAND-ROLLED
  COMPARE-EXCHANGE IS NOT AN ALTERNATIVE TO BE TRADED AWAY -- IT IS
  THE ONLY WAY TO EXPRESS COMPARE-AND-SWAP, AND `amoswap` IS A
  DIFFERENT OPERATION THAT DOES NOT COMPARE.  THE COMPILER EMITS THE
  ONE INSTRUCTION FOR THE ONE-INSTRUCTION OPERATION AND THE LOOP FOR
  THE LOOP-REQUIRING ONE, AND THE CORRECTION IS PRINTED BESIDE THE
  ORIGINAL CLAIM rather than quietly dropped.

  THE `-march` SWEEP, so "the extension is one letter" is a TABLE.
  AND THE ISA STRING IS PRINTED BESIDE IT, because the string
  is what a build system records and a reader checking the record
  will read the string first.

  -march     atomic instructions  library calls  the ISA string                                                                                  
  rv64i      0                    9              rv64i2p1                                                                                        
  rv64im     0                    9              rv64i2p1_m2p0_zmmul1p0                                                                          
  rv64ima    9                    0              rv64i2p1_m2p0_a2p1_zmmul1p0_zaamo1p0_zalrsc1p0                                                  
  rv64imac   9                    0              rv64i2p1_m2p0_a2p1_c2p0_zmmul1p0_zaamo1p0_zalrsc1p0_zca1p0                                      
  rv64imafd  9                    0              rv64i2p1_m2p0_a2p1_f2p2_d2p2_zicsr2p0_zmmul1p0_zaamo1p0_zalrsc1p0                               
  rv64gc     9                    0              rv64i2p1_m2p0_a2p1_f2p2_d2p2_c2p0_zicsr2p0_zifencei2p0_zmmul1p0_zaamo1p0_zalrsc1p0_zca1p0_zcd1p0

  `rv64im` HAS ZERO ATOMIC INSTRUCTIONS AND `rv64ima` HAS 9,
  AND THE ISA STRING RECORDS IT CORRECTLY IN BOTH CASES, WHICH IS
  THE OPPOSITE OF THE FINDING IN THE PREVIOUS COURSE.  THE
  PREVIOUS COURSE SHOWED THAT THE ARCHITECTURE STRING DOES NOT NOTICE
  WHEN YOU USE Zicsr WITHOUT ASKING FOR IT; HERE IT NOTICES THE A
  EXTENSION PERFECTLY, BECAUSE `a` IS A LETTER IN THE REQUEST AND
  ZICSR IS NOT.  THE STRING RECORDS THE REQUEST, AND WHERE A
  FEATURE CAN BE USED WITHOUT BEING NAMED, THE STRING IS A RECORD OF
  INTENT RATHER THAN OF CONTENT -- AND A READER WHO KNOWS WHICH OF
  THE TWO THEY ARE LOOKING AT KNOWS WHAT IT CAN TRUST.

  AND `rv64imac` VERSUS `rv64imafd` is the compression confound from
  the sibling ABI course, kept here deliberately so the table shows
  what a confounded pair looks like: the atomic count is IDENTICAL
  across both, so this pair is CLEAN on the subject being measured, and
  the ABI course's pair was not.

  THE CONFINEMENT OF THE COMPARISON, which is limit 13 in the artifact's
  own words and is worth repeating here because the table is easy to
  over-read: rv64im is the BASE for this experiment and
  `rv64ima` is the base plus ONE letter, so the difference table carries
  exactly one variable.  The four rows above it are NOT the experiment --
  `rv64i` is included to show that even a bare base emits no atomic and
  no call, and `rv64imac` and `rv64imafd` are there to show that adding
  `c` and then `f`/`d` adds nothing to the count.  THE POLICY FOR
  THE FOUR-SYMBOL COMPARISON IS THAT A ROW IS A MEASUREMENT IF IT DIFFERS
  BY ONE LETTER OR SOMETHING ELSE, AND BY ONE LETTER IS THE ONLY VERSION
  OF THAT SENTENCE THAT MEANS ANYTHING.  A DIRTY ROW IS KEPT AND
  LABELLED RATHER THAN DELETED, BECAUSE A TABLE WITH NO CONFOUNDED ROWS IN
  IT IS A TABLE NOBODY HAS LOOKED FOR THEM IN.

==========================================================================
SECTION 7 -- fence -- FOUR FIELDS, FIFTEEN SPELLINGS, ONE DEFINITION
==========================================================================

  THIS IS THE SECTION THE COURSE IS ABOUT, and the finding is not a number but a SHAPE: RISC-V's memory ordering model is not a baseline that barriers override, and it is not a set of access modes. It
  is DEFINED as a relation between two four-bit SETS inside one instruction.

  * x86-64 gives you total store order and lets a barrier do more.  The
    baseline is the common case and the exceptions are opt-in.
  * AArch64 gives every access a MODE -- `ldr`, `ldar`, `stlr` -- so the
    ordering is a property of each access and `dmb` is the coarse barrier.
  * RISC-V gives you a RELAXED baseline, two bits per atomic, and ONE
    instruction whose entire content is "these operations, relative to those".

  THE THREE ARE NOT THREE DEGREES OF THE SAME IDEA. A baseline-and- escape model is a claim about what happens when you do nothing; an access-mode model is a claim about what each instruction means;
  and a predecessor/successor model is a claim about a RELATION BETWEEN TWO SETS. The third is the only one in which the fence is the primitive rather than the escape hatch, and IT IS A DIFFERENCE OF
  FORM: a reader who knows what one fence does on RISC-V can predict what every other ordering operation does, because every other one of them is a fence with a part removed. There is no such
  reduction available in the other two directions, and that asymmetry is the whole point.



  THE FOUR FIELDS, read out of every fence in the corpus.  The word
  comes FIRST and the full spelling second, because the spelling is
  what the encoding implies and the word is what came back.

  word        the spelling      fm      pred       succ       rs1  rd  funct3  the assembler printed
  0x0ff0000f  fence iorw, iorw  0b0000  iorw = 15  iorw = 15  0    0   0                            
  0x0ff0000f  fence iorw, iorw  0b0000  iorw = 15  iorw = 15  0    0   0                            
  0x0220000f  fence r, r        0b0000  r = 2      r = 2      0    0   0       r, r                 
  0x0110000f  fence w, w        0b0000  w = 1      w = 1      0    0   0       w, w                 
  0x0330000f  fence rw, rw      0b0000  rw = 3     rw = 3     0    0   0       rw, rw               
  0x0230000f  fence r, rw       0b0000  r = 2      rw = 3     0    0   0       r, rw                
  0x0320000f  fence rw, r       0b0000  rw = 3     r = 2      0    0   0       rw, r                
  0x0120000f  fence w, r        0b0000  w = 1      r = 2      0    0   0       w, r                 
  0x0210000f  fence r, w        0b0000  r = 2      w = 1      0    0   0       r, w                 
  0x0130000f  fence w, rw       0b0000  w = 1      rw = 3     0    0   0       w, rw                
  0x0310000f  fence rw, w       0b0000  rw = 3     w = 1      0    0   0       rw, w                
  0x0f20000f  fence iorw, r     0b0000  iorw = 15  r = 2      0    0   0       iorw, r              
  0x02f0000f  fence r, iorw     0b0000  r = 2      iorw = 15  0    0   0       r, iorw              
  0x0f10000f  fence iorw, w     0b0000  iorw = 15  w = 1      0    0   0       iorw, w              
  0x01f0000f  fence w, iorw     0b0000  w = 1      iorw = 15  0    0   0       w, iorw              
  0x0880000f  fence i, i        0b0000  i = 8      i = 8      0    0   0       i, i                 

  ... 23 more rows, of which the two non-fence ones are printed below.

  THE TWO WORDS THAT SHARE THE OPCODE AND ARE NOT ORDERING FENCES:

    0x0000100f  fence.i    funct3 = 1, so fm/pred/succ are RESERVED
    0x8330000f  fence.tso  fm = 1000, pred = RW, succ = RW
              and the fm field is the ONLY difference from
              `fence rw, rw`, which is 0x0330000f

  EVERY ONE OF THOSE WORDS HAS rs1 = 0 AND rd = 0, AND THAT IS
  NOT A COINCIDENCE: the manual reserves both fields for finer-grain
  fences in future extensions, says base implementations SHALL ignore
  them, and says standard software SHALL ZERO them.  TWO
  RESERVED FIELDS IN AN INSTRUCTION WHOSE OTHER TWO FIELDS CARRY THE
  WHOLE MODEL IS THE TELL THAT THE ENCODING WAS DESIGNED FOR A
  FUTURE NOBODY HAS BUILT YET.

  THE FIFTEEN SPELLINGS, because "the sets are NAMED and not
  arbitrary" is a claim about the ASSEMBLER and this measures it.

  bits    value  the name  an instruction the assembler emitted
  0b0001  1      w         fence                               
  0b0010  2      r         fence                               
  0b0011  3      rw        fence                               
  0b0100  4      o         fence                               
  0b0101  5      ow        fence                               
  0b0110  6      or        fence                               
  0b0111  7      orw       fence                               
  0b1000  8      i         fence                               
  0b1001  9      iw        fence                               
  0b1010  10     ir        fence                               
  0b1011  11     irw       fence                               
  0b1100  12     io        fence                               
  0b1101  13     iow       fence                               
  0b1110  14     ior       fence                               
  0b1111  15     iorw      fence                               

  15 distinct predecessor codes reached, of the 16 the field can hold.

  AND THE SIXTEENTH IS THE EMPTY SET, WHICH THE ASSEMBLER WILL
  NOT SPELL AT ALL -- `fence` with an empty operand list is REFUSED
  with "too few operands for instruction".  SO THE FIELD HAS SIXTEEN
  VALUES, FIFTEEN OF THEM HAVE NAMES, AND ONE OF THEM IS NOT
  REACHABLE BY NAME.  THAT IS WHY THE TABLE HAS A ROW PRINTED AS A
  HOLE RATHER THAN A GAP: a reader who has not seen the sixteenth
  value cannot know whether it is reserved or merely unused, and
  those are different things to a decoder.

  AND THE FOUR LETTERS ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE, which is the part
  that makes the sets a SET and not a mask: `i` is device input and
  `o` is device output and `r` is a memory read and `w` is a memory
  write, and the manual separates the two DOMAINS deliberately --

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 2.7:
      "The address space is divided by the execution environment
       into memory and I/O domains, and the FENCE instruction
       provides options to order accesses to one or both of these
       two address domains."

  SO `fence r, w` AND `fence io, io` ARE NOT TWO SPELLINGS OF
  ONE THING WITH DIFFERENT AMOUNTS OF IT.  THEY ORDER DIFFERENT
  DOMAINS.  A FOUR-BIT FIELD WHOSE FOUR BITS ARE TWO PAIRS
  FROM TWO INDEPENDENT AXES IS NOT A FOUR-STATE ENUMERATION, IT IS
  TWO TWO-STATE ENUMERATIONS, AND A DECODER THAT TREATS IT AS ONE
  WILL EVENTUALLY HAVE TO SAY "iorw" WHEN IT MEANS "both domains,
  everything".

  35 ordering fences at fm = 0000, 2 fence.tso at fm = 1000, and 2 fence.i at funct3 = 1.  THREE INSTRUCTIONS, ONE OPCODE,
  AND THE ORDERING IS BY funct3 AND BY fm -- so a decoder that
  dispatches on the opcode ALONE gets the wrong one of the three, and
  this course's `m_fence_sets` exists because the inherited decoder
  names two of them correctly for the wrong reason.
  2 fence.i at funct3 = 1.  THREE INSTRUCTIONS, ONE OPCODE,
  AND THE ORDERING IS BY funct3 AND BY fm -- so a decoder that
  dispatches on the opcode ALONE gets the wrong one of the three, and
  this course's `m_fence_sets` exists because the inherited decoder
  names two of them correctly for the wrong reason.

  AND WHAT THE COMPILER CHOSE, because the model being definitional
  is a fact about the ISA and the model being USED is a fact about
  clang 21.1.8, and a course that conflates them is making a claim
  about one from evidence for the other.

  function         instr  fences  what it emitted              
  .Lpcrel_hi0      3      0       NONE                         
  fence_acqrel     2      1       fence.tso (bare)             
  fence_acquire    2      1       fence (r, rw)                
  fence_consume    2      1       fence (r, rw)                
  fence_relaxed    1      0       NONE                         
  fence_release    2      1       fence (rw, w)                
  fence_seqcst     2      1       fence (rw, rw)               
  load_acquire     3      1       fence (r, rw)                
  load_consume     3      1       fence (r, rw)                
  load_relaxed     2      0       NONE                         
  load_seqcst      4      2       fence (rw, rw); fence (r, rw)
  observe          4      1       fence (r, rw)                
  publish          1      1       fence (rw, w)                
  rmw_acqrel       2      0       NONE                         
  rmw_acquire      2      0       NONE                         
  rmw_release      2      0       NONE                         
  rmw_seqcst       2      0       NONE                         
  store_relaxed    2      0       NONE                         
  store_release    3      1       fence (rw, w)                
  store_seqcst     4      2       fence (rw, w); fence (rw, rw)
  sync_full_fence  2      1       fence (rw, rw)               

  8 of the 21 functions emitted NO fence at all.

  AND THE THREE C11 FENCE STRENGTHS, which is where a compiler stops
  choosing and starts TRANSLATING.  The table above says what each
  function emitted; the mapping that produced those words is this,
  and it is worth printing in its own right because the fence it asks
  for is not always the fence whose spelling matches its name:

    __ATOMIC_CONSUME  -> fence r, rw      the same four bits as acquire
    __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE  -> fence r, rw
    __ATOMIC_RELEASE  -> fence rw, w
    __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL  -> fence.tso        a DIFFERENT INSTRUCTION, fm = 1000
    __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST  -> fence rw, rw
    __ATOMIC_RELAXED  -> no instruction at all

  CONSUME AND ACQUIRE ARE THE SAME FOUR BITS, so the C11
  distinction between them is not representable in this encoding and
  the compiler does not pretend otherwise -- it emits one word for
  both.  ACQ_REL EMITS fence.tso AND SEQ_CST EMITS fence rw, rw,
  and the FIRST of those is the one a reader is least likely to expect:
  an acquire-release fence, whose spelling names a barrier, compiles
  to an instruction that is not `fence` at all.  THE THREE
  FORMULATIONS ARE A DIFFERENCE OF FORM AND NOT OF DEGREE, AND THE
  PROOF IS THAT ONE OF THEM HAS A DIFFERENT MNEMONIC.
  `fence_relaxed` IS A BARE `ret`.  A RELAXED FENCE IS NOT A
  NO-OP INSTRUCTION, IT IS THE ABSENCE OF ONE, AND A FUNCTION THAT
  CONSISTS OF A SINGLE RETURN IS A CORRECT COMPILATION OF A FUNCTION
  THAT ASKS FOR NOTHING.  THAT IS ALSO THE FIRST PLACE IN THIS
  COURSE WHERE A COMPILER REMOVED SOMETHING BY BEING CORRECT, AND IT
  IS WORTH NAMING BECAUSE THE PREVIOUS COURSE MEASURED DEAD-CODE
  ELIMINATION AT -O1 AND RETRACTED IT FOR CALLING IT A CONVENTION.
  Here the elision IS the answer: relaxed means no ordering, and the
  encoding for "no ordering" is no instruction.

  AND THE ROW THAT IS THE CROSS-ARCHITECTURE PAYOFF IS
  `load_acquire` AGAINST `load_seqcst`.  AN ACQUIRE LOAD AND A
  SEQUENTIALLY CONSISTENT LOAD ARE THE SAME `lw` -- four bytes, one
  opcode, byte-for-byte -- MEASURED, and the difference is discharged
  by a FENCE beside it.
  ON AArch64 THE SAME TWO C11 OPERATIONS ARE `ldar` AND `ldar`,
  i.e. THE SAME INSTRUCTION, and on x86-64 they are both a plain
  `mov` and the difference is a `mfence` or nothing at all.  
  RISC-V PUTS THE ORDERING IN A SEPARATE INSTRUCTION BECAUSE ITS
  MODEL SAYS ORDERING IS A RELATION BETWEEN TWO SETS AND NOT A
  PROPERTY OF AN ACCESS -- SO A SEQ_CST LOAD IS AN ACQUIRE LOAD AND
  SOMETHING ELSE, RATHER THAN A DIFFERENT ACCESS WITH A DIFFERENT
  NAME.

  AND MEASURED, THE SOMETHING ELSE IS A SECOND FENCE:
  `load_seqcst` emits `fence rw, rw` before the load and `fence r, rw`
  after it, so TWO fences for ONE load, and the section table above
  says why: the extra constraint a seq_cst load carries is on its
  relation to OTHER operations, and it is discharged by ordering the
  neighbours rather than by changing the load.  THIS IS THE
  WHOLE ARGUMENT IN TWO SENTENCES: A LOOP THAT HAS TO BE SPELLED OUT
  BY HAND IS THE PRICE OF A MODEL WHOSE UNIT OF ORDERING IS AN
  INSTRUCTION, AND THE PRICE IS PAID IN WORDS ONCE PER SITE.

==========================================================================
SECTION 8 -- vsetvli AND THE FOUR FIELDS IT CARRIES
==========================================================================

  MEASURED-ON-BYTES, and the finding is the course's sharpest encoding result: ONE FIXED 32-BIT INSTRUCTION DESCRIBES A VECTOR AND THE LENGTH IS NOT IN THIS INSTRUCTION AT ALL.

  That sounds like a defect and it is the design. `vsetvli` carries FOUR things -- the element width (SEW), the register-group multiplier (LMUL), and the two agnostic/undisturbed policies for the tail
  and the mask -- and it carries a FIFTH thing that is not a field but an ABSENCE: the length. The requested length is AVL, it arrives in inst[19:15], and the length that comes out lives in a CSR
  called `vl` that the instruction does not contain.

  SO THE INSTRUCTION IS A DESCRIPTION AND NOT A VALUE: it says what KIND of vector to use, and the machine says how many elements that is. That is how one encoding serves a machine with 128-bit
  vectors and a machine with 1024-bit vectors -- there is nothing in the word to disagree about.


  THE 112 VARIANTS, SWEPT, AND THEN FIVE MORE.  Four element widths x
  seven register-group multipliers x two tail policies x two mask
  policies is 112, with rs1 and rd held constant; the corpus adds five
  AVL forms and three partial-policy forms on top, and the two groups
  are COUNTED SEPARATELY because only the first is a sweep.

  120 vsetvli instructions, 112 DISTINCT zimm values.
  THE MAP IS A BIJECTION OVER WHAT THE ASSEMBLER WILL EMIT --
  EXCEPT FOR 8, AND THE EXCEPTION IS THE POINT.  120 instructions, 112
  DISTINCT VALUES, so 8 instructions SHARE a value with another.  
  EVERY ONE OF THEM IS A PARTIAL-POLICY FORM: `vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m1`
  AND `vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m1, ta` AND `vsetvli t0, a1, e8, m8, tu`
  ARE NOT THREE NEW CONFIGURATIONS.  THEY ARE THE SAME WORDS AS
  THEIR FULLY-SPELLED SIBLINGS, WITH THE MISSING FLAGS DEFAULTED --
  TO `tu, mu`, WHICH IS ALREADY IN THE SWEEP.  A BIJECTION OVER A
  FIELD IS ONLY TRUE OF THE VALUES THE INSTRUCTION CAN CARRY, AND A
  SYNTAX THAT ADDS SPELLINGS DOES NOT ADD VALUES.

  So the whole reachable vtype space of the assembler is 2^6 = 64
  values and the ELEVEN-BIT FIELD IS NOT TIGHT: three of its bits never
  move and two more are only reachable through the register forms of
  the instruction rather than through an immediate.  That is not waste
  -- the reserved bits are what a future extension is entitled to
  spend without moving a bit anyone already uses -- but it is worth
  knowing that a decoder written from the field width alone would
  carry eight impossible cases.

  THE FOUR FIELDS, EACH ISOLATED BY AN XOR.  One variant is the
  base and each row changes exactly one thing.

  the variant      zimm[10:0]   XOR against e8/m1/tu/mu  bits moved  which                 
  e8, m1, tu, mu   00000000000  0x00000000               0                                 
  e16, m1, tu, mu  00000001000  0x00800000               1           bit 23                
  e32, m1, tu, mu  00000010000  0x01000000               1           bit 24                
  e64, m1, tu, mu  00000011000  0x01800000               2           bit 23, bit 24        
  e8, m2, tu, mu   00000000001  0x00100000               1           bit 20                
  e8, m4, tu, mu   00000000010  0x00200000               1           bit 21                
  e8, m8, tu, mu   00000000011  0x00300000               2           bit 20, bit 21        
  e8, mf8, tu, mu  00000000101  0x00500000               2           bit 20, bit 22        
  e8, mf4, tu, mu  00000000110  0x00600000               2           bit 21, bit 22        
  e8, mf2, tu, mu  00000000111  0x00700000               3           bit 20, bit 21, bit 22
  e8, m1, ta, mu   00001000000  0x04000000               1           bit 26                
  e8, m1, tu, ma   00010000000  0x08000000               1           bit 27                
  e8, m1, ta, ma   00011000000  0x0c000000               2           bit 26, bit 27        

  READ OFF THAT TABLE AND THE FIELD MAP IS EXACTLY WHAT THE
  SPECIFICATION DRAWS, AND IT IS WORTH DRAWING OUT BECAUSE THE
  THREE BITS THAT NEVER MOVE ARE THE POINT:

      vma       zimm[7]      one bit, the MASK policy
      vta       zimm[6]      one bit, the TAIL policy
      vsew[2:0] zimm[5:3]    three bits, the ELEMENT WIDTH
      vlmul[2:0]zimm[2:0]    three bits, the REGISTER GROUP
      RESERVED  zimm[10:8]   THREE BITS THAT NO REACHABLE vsetvli USES

  MEASURED: zimm[10:8] is 0b000 in all 120 `vsetvli` in the corpus, of
  which 112 are the sweep and 8 are the AVL and partial-policy forms,
  and the
  table above isolates a field in 6 of its 12 rows with a SINGLE-bit
  XOR -- one per field, four fields -- and the rows that move two or
  three bits are the rows that change a THREE-BIT field at once.

  THREE BITS OF AN ELEVEN-BIT FIELD THAT NO REACHABLE vsetvli USES,
  AND A FIELD MAP MEASURED BY SWEEP RECORDS WHICH BITS MOVE --
  SO THREE BITS THAT NEVER MOVE LOOK EXACTLY LIKE THREE BITS THAT DO.
  THE FIRST VERSION OF THE INHERITED DECODER PRINTED
  `zimm11=0b00011011000` AND ITS COMMENT CLAIMED THE ELEMENT WIDTH
  LIVED IN "THREE BITS OF THE FIELD" -- AND THE COMMENT WAS RIGHT
  ABOUT ITS DATA AND WRONG ABOUT WHAT THE DATA MEANT, BECAUSE IT
  NEVER SAID WHICH THREE.  A COMMENT THAT GIVES THE WIDTH OF A
  FIELD WITHOUT GIVING ITS POSITION IS A COMMENT THAT CANNOT BE
  CHECKED AGAINST THE FIELD.

  The three bits are not WASTED, they are RESERVED, and the manual
  says what they are for --

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 30, Table 48 (vtype register layout):
        XLEN-1  vill      Illegal value if set
        XLEN-2:8  0       Reserved if non-zero
             7  vma       Vector mask agnostic
             6  vta       Vector tail agnostic
           5:3  vsew[2:0] Selected element width (SEW) setting
           2:0  vlmul[2:0]Vector register group multiplier

  THE RESERVED BITS ARE AT THE TOP AND `vill` IS ABOVE THEM, SO
  A FUTURE EXTENSION THAT WANTS A FIELD GETS THE SPACE WITHOUT MOVING
  ANY EXISTING BIT -- WHICH IS WHAT A FIXED 32-BIT ENCODING HAS TO
  BE DESIGNED FOR AND WHAT x86-64 COULD NOT DO, BECAUSE ITS INSTRUCTION
  LENGTH IS VARIABLE AND ITS PREFIXES ARE NOT FIELDS AT ALL.

  AND THE LENGTH IS NOT IN THE INSTRUCTION, which is the finding the
  whole section exists for.  Three measured rows:

  word        the instruction              rd        rs1       what the manual says the AVL encoding means
  0x0d807557  a0, zero, e64, m1, ta, ma    rd = x10  rs1 = x0  AVL = ~0, set vl to VLMAX                  
  0x0d807057  zero, zero, e64, m1, ta, ma  rd = x0   rs1 = x0  AVL = the value in vl, KEEP the old vl     
  0x01107557  a0, zero, e32, m2, tu, mu    rd = x10  rs1 = x0  AVL = ~0, set vl to VLMAX                  

  THREE ROWS, THREE DIFFERENT MEANINGS, AND THE MECHANISM IS
  TWO BITS OF REGISTER NUMBER.  THAT IS THE SAME TWO-BIT
  ARGUMENT AS inst[31:30] CHOOSING WHICH CONFIGURATION INSTRUCTION
  THIS IS, AND IT IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS COURSE THAT A COUPLE OF
  BITS OF REGISTER IDENTITY CARRY A THREE-WAY SELECTION.  A
  REGISTER NUMBER IS NORMALLY A NAME AND HERE IT IS A SWITCH, WHICH
  IS WHY NO DECODER CAN TREAT rd AND rs1 AS OPERANDS WITHOUT READING
  WHICH INSTRUCTION IT IS LOOKING AT FIRST.

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 30, section 31.6.2, Table 49:
        rd    rs1   AVL value              Effect on vl
        -     !x0   Value in x[rs1]        Normal strip mining
        !x0   x0    ~0                     Set vl to VLMAX
        x0    x0    Value in vl register   Keep existing vl

  And the VLMAX row is the one the COMPILER USES, which is the
  measurement in section 10:

    117 of the 120 vsetvli in the corpus name a register, and 3 ask
    for VLMAX by putting x0 in rs1.

  THE THREE CONFIGURATION INSTRUCTIONS, told apart by inst[31:30] and
  by nothing else.  One word of each, the whole spelling, and the two
  bits that choose between them:

