  inst[31:30] IS VSETVLI WHEN IT IS 0b00, 0b10 IS VSETVL, and 0b11
  IS VSETIVLI.  TWO BITS, THREE INSTRUCTIONS, AND NO THIRD
  DISCRIMINATOR ANYWHERE: the other twenty-nine bits of all three are
  laid out the same way, which is why one table above can hold a
  vsetvli and a vsetivli and the only thing that distinguishes them is
  the top two.

  AND inst[19:15] IS A REGISTER IN vsetvli AND IN vsetvl, AND A
  FIVE-BIT IMMEDIATE HERE AND A REGISTER IN BOTH OF ITS SIBLINGS -- that
  is, inst[19:15] IS A FIVE-BIT IMMEDIATE HERE AND A REGISTER IN BOTH
  OF ITS SIBLINGS.  MEASURED: `vsetivli t0, 31` and `vsetivli t0, 0`
  differ by XOR 0x00078000, which is bits 14 and 15 -- the LOW TWO
  BITS of the five-bit field.  A FIELD WHOSE MEANING IS A
  FUNCTION OF A NEIGHBOUR IS NOT A REGISTER, AND THE BASE ISA'S FIELD
  MAP AND THE VECTOR EXTENSION'S DISAGREE ABOUT inst[19:15] AND BOTH
  ARE RIGHT ABOUT THEIR OWN INSTRUCTION.

  AND THE INHERITED DECODER GOT THIS WRONG IN A WAY THAT
  LOOKED RIGHT.  ITS DISPATCH READS `f5 >> 2` ON FUNCT5 =
  inst[31:27], WHICH IS inst[31:29] -- THREE BITS -- AND COMPARES
  AGAINST 0b00, 0b10 AND 0b11.
  0b10 and 0b11.  So it names `vsetvli`, whose inst[31:30] and
  inst[31:29] both happen to be 000, and DECLINES `vsetivli`
  (inst[31:29] = 110, so the test gives 6) and `vsetvl`
  (inst[31:29] = 100, so the test gives 4).  IT NAMES THE ONE
  INSTRUCTION WHERE TWO BITS AND THREE BITS AGREE, AND A MODEL THAT
  IS CORRECT BY COINCIDENCE ON ONE CASE IS A MODEL WHOSE OTHER TWO
  CASES ARE ALSO COINCIDENCES.  THE FIX IS TO READ THE TWO BITS
  THE DOCUMENT SAYS TO READ, AND Section 13's R1 is the whole of it:
  the defect is in the SIBLING's code, it is published rather than
  patched across the course boundary, and the repair is PREPENDED in
  THIS file.

  THE TAIL AND MASK POLICIES, which are the honest half of this
  concept and the reason portable vector code is hard.

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 30, section 31.3.4.3:
        vta vma  Tail Elements      Inactive Elements
         0   0    undisturbed       undisturbed
         0   1    undisturbed       agnostic
         1   0    agnostic          undisturbed
         1   1    agnostic          agnostic

  TWO INDEPENDENT BITS, FOUR COMBINATIONS, AND THE ONE ON THE
  RIGHT IS "AGNOSTIC", WHICH MEANS THE HARDWARE MAY LEAVE THE TAIL
  ALONE OR MAY OVERWRITE IT WITH ALL ONES, AT ITS DISCRETION, AND THE
  MANUAL IS EXPLICIT THAT THE PATTERN IS NOT REQUIRED TO BE
  DETERMINISTIC: "each destination element can be either left
  undisturbed or overwritten with 1s, in any combination, and the
  pattern ... is not required to be deterministic when the
  instruction is executed with the same inputs."

  THAT IS A LIBRARY CONTRACT MADE OF BITS, AND IT IS THE ONE
  PLACE IN THE VECTOR EXTENSION WHERE THE ANSWER IS "THE HARDWARE MAY
  DO EITHER".  PORTABLE VECTOR CODE MUST THEREFORE CHOOSE
  `tu`/`mu` UNLESS IT KNOWS THE TARGET, AND EVERY PERFORMANCE-MINDED
  VECTOR LOOP WANTS `ta`/`ma`, SO THE FAST VERSION IS NOT THE
  PORTABLE ONE AND THE ENCODING IS WHERE THAT DECISION IS VISIBLE.

  AND WHAT THE ASSEMBLER DOES WHEN THE FLAGS ARE OMITTED, which is the
  one measurement in this section that is about the TOOLCHAIN rather
  than about the manual:


  THE SPECIFICATION SAYS THE FLAGS ARE MANDATORY AND THAT
  OMITTING THEM IS DEPRECATED -- AND THE ASSEMBLER ACCEPTS THEM AND
  DEFAULTS TO `tu, mu`, WHICH IS THE CONSERVATIVE POLICY.  THE
  SPECIFICATION ALSO SAYS "THE DEFAULT SHOULD PERHAPS BE
  TAIL-AGNOSTIC/MASK-AGNOSTIC", AND THE ASSEMBLER DISAGREED WITH THE
  SPECIFICATION'S OWN SUGGESTION AND KEPT THE HISTORICAL DEFAULT.
  THAT IS THE RIGHT CHOICE AND IT IS WORTH NAMING WHY: A DEFAULT
  THAT CHANGES BEHAVIOUR SILENTLY IS WORSE THAN A DEPRECATION, AND A
  PROGRAM THAT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT ITS TAIL SHOULD NOT GET THE FAST
  ONE.

  And the reason the policies EXIST is also in the manual, and it is
  a microarchitectural reason rather than a language one:

    "The agnostic policy was added to accommodate machines with
     vector register renaming.  With an undisturbed policy, all
     elements would have to be read from the old physical
     destination vector register to be copied into the new one."

  MEASURED, in the corpus: how does the compiler choose?

  tail  mask  how many of the corpus's vsetvli
  ta    ma    31                              
  ta    mu    29                              
  tu    ma    28                              
  tu    mu    32                              

  31 of 120 are tail-AGNOSTIC and mask-AGNOSTIC.

  AND THAT IS THE FAST CHOICE, NOT THE PORTABLE ONE, SO THE
  COMPILER IS MAKING A NON-PORTABLE CHOICE IN ITS DEFAULT OUTPUT AND
  THE ONLY WAY A READER LEARNS THAT IS BY DECODING TWO BITS.  
  THE PORTABILITY PROBLEM IN THE VECTOR EXTENSION IS NOT THE
  INSTRUCTION SET -- IT IS TWO BITS THAT THE SPECIFICATION DELIBERATELY
  LEAVES UNDETERMINED, AND A COMPILER THAT PICKS THE FAST ONE BY
  DEFAULT.

  AND THE FLAGS ARE MANDATORY NOW, which the assembler enforces and
  which is a small history worth one paragraph because it is visible
  in a REFUSAL:

    vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m1                  ACCEPTED
    vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m1, ta              ACCEPTED
    vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m1, xx, ma          REFUSED: operand must be e[8|16|32|64],m[1|2|4|8|f2|f4|f8],[t
    vsetvli t0, a1, e1024, m1, ta, ma        REFUSED: operand must be e[8|16|32|64],m[1|2|4|8|f2|f4|f8],[t
    vsetvli t0, a1, e32, m16, ta, ma         REFUSED: operand must be e[8|16|32|64],m[1|2|4|8|f2|f4|f8],[t

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 30, section 31.3.4.3: "The assembly syntax
    adds TWO MANDATORY FLAGS to the vsetvli instruction ... Prior to
    v0.9, when these flags were not specified on a vsetvli, they
    defaulted to mask-undisturbed/tail-undisturbed.  The use of
    vsetvli without these flags is deprecated."

  SO THE DEFAULTS CHANGED AND THE CHANGE WAS MADE BY REMOVING THE
  DEFAULT, WHICH IS A BETTER ANSWER THAN CHANGING IT: a program that
  SAYS NOTHING now gets a diagnostic, and a program that says `tu`
  means it.  THAT IS THE ONLY WAY TWO SPECIFICATION VERSIONS CAN
  DISAGREE WITHOUT A COMPILER BEING UNABLE TO TELL WHICH IS WHICH.

==========================================================================
SECTION 9 -- THE MASK IS ONE BIT IN THREE GROUPS -- AND THE REGISTER IS NOT IN THE WORD
==========================================================================

  MEASURED-ON-BYTES, and this is the section with the cleanest table in the course: ELEVEN PAIRS OF INSTRUCTIONS, EACH PAIR THE SAME INSTRUCTION WITH AND WITHOUT A MASK, AND EVERY XOR IS 0x02000000.

  AND ELEVEN IS NOT TWENTY-TWO, WHICH IS HOW THE COURSE WAS FIRST WRITTEN. The first draft of this section claimed twenty-two pairs, having counted three arithmetic forms at TWO widths when the corpus
  contains one width per arithmetic form. A COUNT THAT CANNOT COME OUT THE SAME TWICE IS A COUNT OF SOMETHING ELSE, AND THE CHEAPEST TEST FOR IT IS TO PRINT THE TABLE AND COUNT THE ROWS -- WHICH THE
  FIRST DRAFT DID NOT, BECAUSE IT PRINTED THE CLAIM INSTEAD OF THE EVIDENCE FOR IT.

  One bit. In an arithmetic instruction, in a unit-stride load and in a unit-stride store. AND THE MASK REGISTER ITSELF IS NOT IN THE ENCODING: `vm` is a single bit that says "use v0", and v0 is the
  ONLY mask register the encoding can name. There is no vd field for the mask and no register field for it, because the extension has one mask register.

  That is a different design from x86-64, where a mask is an ordinary vector register (`vle` with a mask pointer in `k1`), and a different one from NEON predication, where the mask IS the vector
  register.



  THE ELEVEN XORs.  Each row is a pair of ADJACENT instructions in
  vec.s -- unmasked, then masked -- XORed.

  instruction  the masked form    XOR         bits  which 
  vadd.vv      v1, v2, v3, v0.t   0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vadd.vi      v1, v2, 0x7, v0.t  0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vadd.vx      v1, v2, a0, v0.t   0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vle8.v       v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vle16.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vle32.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vle64.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vse8.v       v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vse16.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vse32.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25
  vse64.v      v1, (a0), v0.t     0x02000000  1     bit 25

  11 pairs, 11 of them XOR to exactly 0x02000000, 0 exceptions.

  ONE BIT, ELEVEN TIMES, IN THREE INSTRUCTION GROUPS.
  THE MASK
  IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE VECTOR EXTENSION OR OF THE DATA PATH OR OF
  ONE INSTRUCTION CLASS -- IT IS A PROPERTY OF A WORD, AND THE WORD
  IS THE ONLY THING THE THREE GROUPS SHARE.

  AND THE BIT IS 25, WHICH IS ALSO WHERE `rl` LIVES IN AN AMO.  
  BIT 25 IS "RELEASE" IN AN ATOMIC AND "MASK AGNOSTIC" IN A VECTOR
  INSTRUCTION, AND THE TWO MEANINGS ARE IN DIFFERENT OPCODES SO THEY
  NEVER COLLIDE.  A BIT POSITION IS A PROPERTY OF AN
  INSTRUCTION GROUP AND NOT OF AN ARCHITECTURE -- WHICH IS THE SAME
  FINDING THE AARCH64 COURSE REACHED FROM THE OTHER DIRECTION, WHERE
  BIT 23 IS "128-BIT ATOMIC" IN ONE GROUP AND "ACQUIRE" IN ANOTHER,
  AND THAT FINDING COST THAT COURSE A RETRACTION.

  THE MASK REGISTER IS v0 AND ONLY v0, and the assembler REFUSES the
  obvious alternative, which is a better measurement than a claim:

    vadd.vv v1, v2, v3, v0.t         ACCEPTED, 0x002180d7
    vadd.vv v1, v2, v3, v1.t         REFUSED: operand must be v0.t
    vle32.v v1, (a0), v0.t           ACCEPTED, 0x00056087
    vle32.v v1, (a0), v1.t           REFUSED: operand must be v0.t
    vmv.v.i v1, 5, v0.t              REFUSED: invalid operand for instruction
    vl2r.v v8, (a0), v0.t            REFUSED: invalid operand for instruction

  "OPERAND MUST BE v0.t" IS A REFUSAL THAT STATES A PROPERTY OF
  THE ENCODING IN THE DIAGNOSTIC.  THE ASSEMBLER IS TELLING
  THE PROGRAMMER SOMETHING TRUE ABOUT THE HARDWARE -- ONE MASK
  REGISTER EXISTS AND IT IS v0 -- AND IT IS DOING IT IN A FORM THAT
  CANNOT BE MISREAD AS A SPELLING RULE.

  So the masked form is the SAME INSTRUCTION with one bit changed and
  a register the encoding does not name.  A MASK REGISTER THAT
  IS NOT IN THE ENCODING IS WHY A DECODER CAN NAME TWICE AS MANY
  VECTOR INSTRUCTIONS AS IT HAS OPERANDS TO PRINT: the space of
  (instruction, mask register) PAIRS is not in the word, and a
  decoder that tried to model it would find 32 times as many cases as
  there are bits.

  AND `v0` IS A DESTINATION AS WELL AS A SOURCE, which is measured by
  the corpus containing mask PRODUCERS:

  instruction  word        vd  operands   
  vmsne.vi     0x66103057  0   v0, v1, 0x0
  vmsne.vi     0x6611b057  0   v0, v1, 0x3
  vlm.v        0x02b50107  2   v2, (a0)   

  AND HERE IS THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG IN THE OTHER
  DIRECTION, SO IT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED.  A
  MASK-PRODUCING INSTRUCTION CAN WRITE ANY VECTOR REGISTER, NOT ONLY
  v0 -- MEASURED: `vmsne.vi v1, v2, 0` ASSEMBLES -- AND THE ONLY ONE OF
  THOSE THAT CAN THEN BE USED AS A MASK IS v0.  SO THE CONSTRAINT
  IS NOT "MASKS ARE v0" BUT "MASKS ARE v0 AND MASKS ARE PRODUCED BY
  ORDINARY VECTOR REGISTERS", WHICH IS A WEAKER CLAIM AND THE CORRECT
  ONE.  A REGISTER ALLOCATOR MUST THEREFORE KEEP v0 OUT OF THE
  GENERAL POOL -- not because writing it is illegal, but because
  anything it writes will eventually be used as a mask, and that is a
  CONSTRAINT ON THE ALLOCATOR AND NOT ON THE INSTRUCTION.

  The first draft of this section said "v0 is the only mask register
  the encoding can name, therefore a register allocator cannot treat
  a mask as a distinct kind of thing" -- and the first half is right
  and the inference is wrong, because a mask REGISTER and a mask
  PRODUCER are different things and the assembler accepts a producer
  writing any register.  A TRUE PREMISE WITH A FALSE INFERENCE
  IS HARDER TO CATCH THAN A FALSE PREMISE, BECAUSE THE FALSE HALF IS
  THE SECOND HALF.

  AND THE REGISTER GROUP, which is what LMUL buys and which is the
  other half of what a `vsetvli` carries.

  instruction  word        nf at [31:29]  registers  vs2/vd  operands
  vl1r.v       0x02850087  0b000          1          8       v1, (a0)
  vl2r.v       0x22850407  0b001          2          8       v8, (a0)
  vl4r.v       0x62850407  0b011          4          8       v8, (a0)
  vl8r.v       0xe2850407  0b111          8          8       v8, (a0)
  vl2re64.v    0x22857407  0b001          2          8       v8, (a0)
  vs1r.v       0x028500a7  0b000          1          8       v1, (a0)
  vs2r.v       0x22850427  0b001          2          8       v8, (a0)
  vs4r.v       0x62850427  0b011          4          8       v8, (a0)
  vs8r.v       0xe2850427  0b111          8          8       v8, (a0)

  nf IS THREE BITS AT THE TOP OF THE WORD AND IT IS THE NUMBER
  OF REGISTERS MINUS ONE: 0, 1, 3 and 7 for one, two, four and eight.
  A GROUP OF FOUR IS NOT A WIDER REGISTER.  IT IS FOUR REGISTERS
  THE INSTRUCTION ADDRESSES TOGETHER, AND THE ASSEMBLER PROVES IT BY
  REFUSING A GROUP THAT DOES NOT START ALIGNED:

    vl2r.v v1, (a0)          REFUSED
    vl2r.v v8, (a0)          ACCEPTED
    vl4r.v v8, (a0)          ACCEPTED
    vl8r.v v8, (a0)          ACCEPTED
    vs2r.v v1, (a0)          REFUSED
    vs2r.v v8, (a0)          ACCEPTED

  SO LMUL IS NOT A SCALAR AND IT IS NOT A WIDER REGISTER.  IT
  IS AN ALIGNMENT CONSTRAINT ON A REGISTER ALLOCATOR, AND THE ENCODER
  ENFORCES IT BY REFUSING A WORD.  A SCALAR ENABLES LOOP
  UNROLLING WITH NO ALIGNMENT CONSTRAINT, SO THE THREE-BIT nf FIELD
  IS THE PRICE OF NOT HAVING A LOWER BOUND ON WHAT A VECTOR REGISTER
  IS.

  MEASURED, and the compiler pays it: the `vsum_d` loop in section 10
  uses LMUL = 2 and emits `vl2re64.v v8` and `vfredosum.vs`, so the
  group start is v8 rather than v0 -- and v0 is taken, because the
  reduction needs it.  A REGISTER GROUP THAT MUST START ON A
  BOUNDARY AND A RESERVED REGISTER AT ZERO ARE THE SAME CONSTRAINT
  SEEN FROM TWO SIDES.

==========================================================================
SECTION 10 -- WHAT THE COMPILER EMITS, AND WHAT IT CANNOT RUN
==========================================================================

  MEASURED, and this section is where a reader who has just come from `simd` will be most disoriented, so the disorientation is named up front.

  `simd` measured a speedup. `smp` measured one too. `a64simd` measured three speedups of its own and led with the refusal to invent replacements. THIS COURSE HAS NO TIMING OF ANY KIND AND THE REASON
  IS NOT MODESTY: there is no RISC-V machine, no emulator and no RISC-V linker on this host, so not one of the instructions in section 8 has ever been EXECUTED.

  What replaces a ratio is a count, and a count is a weaker thing in a specific way that is worth stating precisely rather than apologising for. AN INSTRUCTION COUNT DOES NOT KNOW WHETHER THE
  INSTRUCTION IS FAST. It knows what the compiler DID, which is a fact, and it does not know what the hardware did with it, which was the other half.

  The design of the experiment is the point, so it is worth one sentence: both builds keep the `c` extension and differ ONLY in the `v`. An earlier draft compared `rv64g` against `rv64gcv` and had to
  retract the number, because dropping `c` as well as adding `v` confounds two variables -- which is the lesson the sibling ABI course recorded as its own R7.


  THE LOOP COUNTS.  Four functions, four optimisation levels, with
  and without the `v`.  Instruction counts and vector counts.

  function  -O0 no v  -O1 no v  -O2 no v  -O3 no v  -Os no v  -O0 +v  -O1 +v  -O2 +v  -O3 +v  -Os +v 
  vadd_d    36        12        12        12        12        37      12      53 +4v  53 +4v  12     
  vmac_l    37        13        13        13        13        38      13      47 +7v  47 +7v  29 +12v
  vmul_s8   35        11        11        11        11        36      11      51 +4v  51 +4v  11     
  vsum_d    35        13        13        13        12        36      13      40 +4v  40 +4v  29 +9v 

  -O1 DOES NOT VECTORISE THIS CORPUS AT ALL, -Os VECTORISES TWO
  OF THE FOUR FUNCTIONS, AND -O2 AND -O3 VECTORISE ALL FOUR.
  MEASURED: 0 vector instructions at -O1, 19 at -O2, 19 at -O3 and
  21 at -Os.  AND -Os IS SMALLER THAN -O2, WHICH IS NOT THE
  USUAL STORY: -Os IS A SIZE OPTIMISATION AND THE VECTORISER IS A
  PROFITABILITY DECISION, SO THE TWO DISAGREE.  A LEVEL OF -O
  NAMES AN OPTIMISATION GOAL AND NOT A CAPABILITY, AND THE VECTORISER
  IS THE MOST CAPABILITY-SENSITIVE PASS IN THE COMPILER.

  CODE SIZE: 191 instructions at -O2 against 81 at -Os, over the
  same four functions.  SO THE SIZE GOAL AND THE VECTOR GOAL
  DISAGREE IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS ON THE SAME FOUR FUNCTIONS, AND
  NEITHER -O LEVEL IS THE OTHER ONE WITH A DIFFERENT NAME.

  AND -O2 AND -O3 ARE THE SAME ASSEMBLY, which is a measurement and
  not an expectation:

    vadd_d     IDENTICAL
    vmac_l     IDENTICAL
    vmul_s8    IDENTICAL
    vsum_d     IDENTICAL

  SO THERE IS NOTHING TO COMPARE BETWEEN -O2 AND -O3 ON THIS
  CORPUS, AND A READER WHO REPORTS A DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THEM HAS
  FOUND A DIFFERENT CORPUS.  TWO OPTIMISATION LEVELS THAT
  AGREE ON A FILE ARE A MEASUREMENT ABOUT THE FILE AND NOT ABOUT THE
  LEVELS.

  AND THE ONE THAT PROVES THE LENGTH IS NOT IN THE INSTRUCTION: the
  compiler READS IT FROM A CSR.  MEASURED:

    -O2  vadd_d   0xc22028f3  csrr a7, vlenb
    -O2  vmac_l   0xc22026f3  csrr a3, vlenb
    -O2  vmul_s8  0xc2202873  csrr a6, vlenb
    -O2  vsum_d   0xc22026f3  csrr a3, vlenb
    -Os  vmac_l   0xc22022f3  csrr t0, vlenb
    -Os  vsum_d   0xc22028f3  csrr a7, vlenb

  `csrr a7, vlenb` -- A CSR READ, AN ORDINARY Zicsr INSTRUCTION,
  THE SAME ENCODING AS `csrr a7, cycle` WITH A DIFFERENT NUMBER.  AND
  THE VECTOR LENGTH IS NOT IN ANY VECTOR INSTRUCTION, BECAUSE IT IS A
  PROPERTY OF THE IMPLEMENTATION AND THE IMPLEMENTATION PUBLISHES IT
  IN A REGISTER.

    rv32-unpriv, chapter 30, section 31.3.6: "The XLEN-bit-wide
    read-only CSR vlenb holds the value VLEN/8, i.e., the vector
    register length in BYTES.  The value in vlenb is a design-time
    constant in any implementation."

  The CSR number is measurable, and it is in the RANGE THE PRIVILEGED
  COURSE ALREADY DECOMPOSED, which is a nice piece of the section
  fitting together:

    0xc22028f3  csrr a7, vlenb
      csr = inst[31:20] = 0xc22   funct3 = 2 (CSRRs)   rd = 17
      csr[11:10] = 0b11  accessibility: READ-ONLY
      csr[9:8]   = 0b00  lowest privilege: U
      the same three fields the privileged course printed for `mtvec`

  SO A PROGRAM CAN FIND OUT HOW WIDE THE VECTOR UNIT IS BY
  READING A CSR, AND THE ANSWER IS A RUNTIME VALUE FROM A READ-ONLY
  REGISTER -- WHICH IS EXACTLY WHAT "the length is a property of the
  implementation" MEANS IN AN ENCODING.  A FIXED 32-BIT
  INSTRUCTION DESCRIBES A VECTOR OF ANY LENGTH BECAUSE THE LENGTH IS
  NOT IN THE INSTRUCTION AND IS PUBLISHED ELSEWHERE.

  AND THE TAIL, which is where the runtime length becomes CODE.  The
  scalar prologue is not dead weight -- IT IS THE REMAINDER, and it is
  the loop the vector loop cannot run:

    -O2     vadd_d     53 instr: 47 scalar, 1 vsetvli, 4 vector data
                    the vector data: vl2re64.v vl2re64.v vfadd.vv vs2r.v
    -O2     vmac_l     47 instr: 37 scalar, 1 vsetvli, 7 vector data
                    the vector data: vmv.v.i vl2re64.v vl2re64.v vmacc.vv vmv.s.x vredsum.vs vmv.x.s
    -O2     vmul_s8    51 instr: 45 scalar, 1 vsetvli, 4 vector data
                    the vector data: vl2r.v vl2r.v vmul.vv vs2r.v
    -O2     vsum_d     40 instr: 32 scalar, 2 vsetvli, 4 vector data
                    the vector data: vmv.s.x vl2re64.v vfredosum.vs vfmv.f.s

  THE VECTOR LOOP IS STRAIGHT-LINE.  There is no branch inside
  it and no tail check: the compiler has already computed the trip
  count as a multiple of the elements per vector, so the vector body
  runs a whole number of times and the REMAINDER IS A SEPARATE SCALAR
  LOOP.  IT IS THE REMAINDER, NOT DEAD WEIGHT, and the reason it
  is worth this much space is that IT IS THE TAIL-HANDLING CODE THE
  SECTION PROMISED.  AND IT IS NOT IN THE VECTOR LOOP AT ALL --
  IT IS BESIDE IT, AND THAT IS WHY THE SCALAR COUNT IS NOT ZERO EVEN
  WHEN THERE ARE VECTOR INSTRUCTIONS.  A REMAINDER HANDLED INSIDE
  THE VECTOR LOOP AND A REMAINDER HANDLED BESIDE IT ARE DIFFERENT
  PROGRAMS WITH THE SAME OUTPUT, AND ONLY THE SECOND ONE CAN IGNORE
  WHAT THE TAIL POLICY DOES.

  AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE PORTABILITY QUESTION FROM SECTION
  8 WITHOUT BEING AN ANSWER TO IT.  The tail is handled by arithmetic
  on the trip count and a scalar loop, which works on EVERY
  IMPLEMENTATION REGARDLESS OF WHETHER `ta` OR `tu` WAS REQUESTED --
  AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY REQUESTING `ta` IS SAFE HERE AND NOT SAFE
  EVERYWHERE.  THE COMPILER GETS PORTABILITY NOT FROM THE POLICY
  BITS BUT FROM NOT DEPENDING ON THE TAIL, AND A READER WHO SEES
  `ta, ma` IN THE ENCODING HAS LEARNED NOTHING ABOUT WHICH OF THE TWO
  STRATEGIES WAS USED.

  WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT SAY, and it is most of what a reader wants:

    * That any vector instruction is FASTER than the scalar loop it replaces.  No instruction in this course has run and no timing exists anywhere in this file.
    * What VLEN IS on any real machine.  `vlenb` is read by the compiler and its VALUE is in no file here; a VLEN of 128 and a VLEN of 1024 would both produce this assembly.
    * Whether the vectorised loop is CORRECT.  Nothing was executed, so no output was compared against anything.
    * Whether `ta` on this hardware overwrites the tail with ones or leaves it alone.  The manual permits both and this host has no vector hardware to be permissive or not on.
    * How much of the element count per iteration depends on VLEN.  The compiler emits a division and a multiply by a value read at run time, which is a fact about the CODE and not about the answer.

==========================================================================
SECTION 11 -- TWO READERS ON THE SAME BYTES
==========================================================================

  The decoder that produced sections 2 through 10 is checked by a second reader -- `llvm-objdump-21` -- against this file's own decoder, over the whole corpus.

  AND BOTH READERS COME FROM ONE LLVM TREE, because `clang` assembled the corpus and GNU binutils has no RISC-V target on this host. So what this section establishes is "this decoder and one other
  piece of software agree on what the bytes mean" -- and NOT "either agrees with silicon". That is a limit and it is limit 11, and it is the reason the two-reader result is a result about software.


    2197  instructions the second reader printed
    2147  of them this file NAMES
      50  unmodelled -- counted, never dropped
       0  where the two readers disagree on the LENGTH

       0  DISAGREE

  THE CLASSIFIER, run on every run:

    NOTHING TO CLASSIFY -- and that is a run where the classifier
    RAN and found nothing, which is not the same as a run where
    it never ran, and the difference is why it lives on the main
    path rather than in the failure branch.

  THE FIRE TABLE, pre-seeded so a rule that never fires is a row with
  a zero rather than an absence:

  normalisation rule                                           times it fired  verdict    
  negative immediate, decimal on both sides                    247             fired      
  c.li and li are one instruction                              39              fired      
  vsetivli immediate, decimal against 0x-hex                   6               fired      
  bare fence and fence with explicit sets are one instruction  2               fired      
  fence.tso with and without its sets is one instruction       7               fired      
  vsetvli suffix order, aqrl and rl-aq are one spelling        0               NEVER FIRED

    6 rules, 5 fired, 1 matched nothing

  AND THE INHERITED TABLE beside it, because this course BORROWS the
  normaliser rather than forking it and a borrowed table with no
  numbers next to it is a table a reader cannot check:

    47 rules INHERITED from rvdec.py, 28 fired on this corpus and 19
    are DEAD -- which is EXPECTED and is not a defect: this corpus is
    one third vector and two thirds C, so the rules about floating
    point and about the A64 forms have nothing to bite on.  A
    DEAD RULE IN AN INHERITED TABLE IS A RULE FOR A CORPUS THIS
    COURSE DOES NOT HAVE, AND THE INVERTED CONDITION IS THE CORRECT
    ONE HERE: THIS COURSE'S OWN SIX RULES ARE PRE-SEEDED AND THE
    HEALTHY STATE IS ZERO DEAD AMONG THEM.

  AND THE FIRE TABLE IS WHAT MAKES "ZERO DISAGREEMENTS" A RESULT
  RATHER THAN AN ABSENCE, BECAUSE IT SHOWS THE COMPARISON DID
  SOMETHING.  WITHOUT THE FIRST RULE, `fence` AND `fence rw, rw`
  WOULD BE COUNTED AS DISAGREEMENTS -- A SPELLING DIFFERENCE AND NOT A
  MISREAD -- AND THE DISAGREEMENT COUNT WOULD BE A NUMBER ABOUT HOW
  THE ASSEMBLER PRINTS THINGS RATHER THAN ABOUT WHETHER EITHER READER
  IS RIGHT.

  THE UNMODELLED COUNT IS PRINTED BESIDE THE DISAGREEMENT COUNT and
  not in a footnote, for the reason the sibling course recorded as a
  retraction: a model can be removed, become unreachable, or be
  shadowed, and every word it owned can end up named by something
  else at the same address -- so a check that watched only the
  disagreement count would call the result progress when it is the
  reverse of progress.  A CROSS-CHECK THAT COUNTS AGREEMENTS AND
  ONE THAT COUNTS HOLES ARE MEASURING DIFFERENT THINGS, AND PRINTING
  ONLY ONE OF THEM IS HOW A DECODER DELETES A MODEL WITHOUT ANYBODY
  NOTICING.

  50 UNMODELLED WORDS IS AN HONEST NUMBER FOR THIS CORPUS: THE
  VECTOR EXTENSION HAS SEVERAL HUNDRED ENCODINGS AND THIS FILE
  NAMES THE THREE CONFIGURATION INSTRUCTIONS, THE MASK BIT IN THREE
  GROUPS AND THE REGISTER GROUP, AND COUNTS THE REST.  A
  DECODER THAT NAMED EVERY VECTOR WORD AND GOT ONE WRONG WOULD BE A
  WORSE DECODER THAN ONE THAT SAYS WHICH HALF OF THE EXTENSION IT
  IMPLEMENTS -- AND THE HALF IT IMPLEMENTS IS THE HALF THE FOUR
  CONCEPT PAGES MAKE CLAIMS ABOUT.

  AND THE ORDER OF THOSE RULES IS ITSELF A FINDING, so it is printed
  rather than left in a comment: A NORMALISER'S RULES HAVE AN
  ORDER AND THE ORDER IS INVISIBLE IN THE RULES THEMSELVES.  Three of
  this course's own rules run BEFORE the inherited normaliser, on the
  RAW printed text, and two run AFTER it, on its OUTPUT -- and the
  reason is a shape rather than a preference.  THE INHERITED
  FUNCTION HANDLES A NEGATIVE IMMEDIATE INCONSISTENTLY BETWEEN ITS OWN
  TWO RULES: IT PRINTS `li a3, -1` AS HEX AND `c.li a3, -1` IN DECIMAL,
  SO A CORRECTION APPLIED AFTER IT CANNOT UNDO THE DAMAGE AND A RULE
  WRITTEN AGAINST ITS OUTPUT MATCHES NOTHING.  THIS IS THE SIXTH
  TIME THIS COLLECTION HAS PAID FOR A NORMALISATION RULE THAT MATCHED
  NOTHING, AND THE FIRE TABLE ABOVE IS HOW THE NEXT ONE GETS CAUGHT:
  a rule with a zero beside it is visible and a rule that was never
  written is not.  THE SECOND REVERSED SIDE IS THE OTHER HALF OF
  THE SAME ARGUMENT: THE INHERITED FUNCTION REMOVES THE SPACE BETWEEN A
  MNEMONIC AND ITS FIRST OPERAND, SO `fence iorw, iorw` ARRIVES AS
  `fenceiorw,iorw` AND A RULE MATCHING `\s+` MATCHES NOTHING ON ALL
  113 OF THEM.

  AND THE FIRE TABLE IS A TABLE OF NORMALISATION, NOT OF
  MODELLING -- which is worth saying because the two are easy to
  confuse, and a reader who has just read 50 unmodelled words could
  reasonably think the table above is about them.  It is not: those
  words are unmodelled because no model claims them, and the table
  counts fixes applied to words that WERE named.

  TWO REPAIRS TO THE INHERITED DECODER, and both are visible here
  because a repair nobody can see is a repair nobody can check:

  THE FIRST IS THE LOAD-MODE FIELD.  A unit-stride load and a
  WHOLE-REGISTER load differ by inst[24:20] -- lumop -- and the
  inherited decoder read only the width, so it said `vle8.v` where the
  second reader said `vl1r.v`, and lumop = 01000 IS THE WHOLE-REGISTER
  FORM.  ONE FIVE-BIT FIELD IN A UNIT-STRIDE LOAD CARRIES THREE
  INSTRUCTION NAMES, AND A DECODER THAT READS ONLY THE WIDTH GETS ALL
  ELEVEN WRONG WHILE BEING RIGHT ABOUT EVERY FIELD IT DID READ.  
  INCOMPLETE IS HARDER TO NOTICE THAN WRONG, BECAUSE NOTHING IN THE
  OUTPUT LOOKS BROKEN -- IT LOOKS LIKE A SPELLING DIFFERENCE.

  THE SECOND IS THE DISPATCH GUARD AGAINST THE CONFIGURATION
  INSTRUCTIONS, and it is here because the first version of this
  course's own vector model did not have it.  `vsetvli t0, a1, e8,
  mf8, ta, ma` is 0x0c55f2d7, and its funct6 -- inst[31:26] -- is
  0b000011, WHICH IS OPFVV.  A MODEL THAT DISPATCHES ON THE
  funct6 ALONE CLAIMS THE CONFIGURATION INSTRUCTIONS, and the first run
  of the cross-check reported 112 of them as `vadd.vi v5, v5, 11,
  v0.t`.  THE FIX IS TO READ THE COARSEST SELECTOR FIRST: the
  guard is inst[31:30] == 0b01, because the OP-V DATA encoding has
  bits[31:30] = 01 and the three configuration instructions have 00, 10
  and 11.  A MODEL MUST READ THE COARSEST SELECTOR BEFORE THE
  FINEST ONE, AND A MODEL THAT SKIPS A LEVEL IN THE DISPATCH WILL
  EVENTUALLY CLAIM A WORD AT THE FINER LEVEL THAT IS NOT AT THE
  COARSER ONE.  THIS IS THE ENCODING COURSE'S OWN FINDING, AND
  IT BIT THIS FILE TWICE: the inherited decoder read THREE bits where
  the encoding has TWO, and this model read SIX where it had to read
  TWO FIRST.

==========================================================================
SECTION 12 -- FOUR POISONS, AND EACH ONE HAS TO MOVE ITS OWN NUMBER
==========================================================================

  A control that cannot be made to fail is a comment that says the word POISONED. THIS COLLECTION HAS PAID FOR THAT LESSON REPEATEDLY: THE AARCH64 DATA-PATH COURSE'S CROSS-CHECK REPORTED ZERO
  DISAGREEMENTS OVER A CORPUS WHERE EIGHTY-FIVE INSTRUCTIONS GENUINELY DISAGREED, AND EVERY ONE OF THE FOUR BUGS HAD MADE THE COMPARISON VACUOUS RATHER THAN WRONG. NOT ONE PRINTED A BAD WORD. THEY ALL
  STOPPED THE COMPARISON HAPPENING.

  So each of the four below names the number it tests, runs the SAME loop over the SAME bytes, prints the DELTA as a number, and prints [POISON FAILED] if the delta is zero.


  POISON 1 -- remove THIS COURSE'S OWN `m_vector_mask`, the model
              that names the vector DATA path.  It claims TWO
              numbers -- the NAMED count and the UNMODELLED count --
              and it claims a ZERO for the DISAGREEMENT count, which
              is the interesting part: removing this model creates a
              HOLE and not a WRONG ANSWER, because there is nothing
              behind it in the dispatch that can name a vector data
              word.  A HOLE AND A WRONG ANSWER LOOK IDENTICAL IN
              A CROSS-CHECK THAT ONLY COUNTS AGREEMENTS, AND THE POISON
              BELOW IS THE OTHER ONE.

    BEFORE: 2147 named, 0 disagree, 50 unmodelled
    AFTER : 2100 named, 0 disagree, 97 unmodelled
    DELTA: named -47, disagreements +0, unmodelled +47
    VERDICT: POISON 1 FIRED

  THE UNMODELLED COUNT RISES BY EXACTLY THE NUMBER OF WORDS THE MODEL OWNED, WHICH IS THE PROOF THAT IT OWNED THEM. A MODEL THAT OWNS NOTHING IN A CORPUS CANNOT BE POISONED, AND THAT IS WHY THE VICTIM
  IS CHOSEN FROM THE CORPUS AND NOT FROM A LIST OF INTERESTING MODELS: this file's `m_amo_order` owns far fewer words and `m_fence_sets` fewer still, so they are poison 2's victim instead.


  POISON 2 -- remove `m_amo_order`, the model that READS inst[26] and
              inst[25].  The inherited `a_amo` is still in the
              dispatch and it does not read those bits, so removing
              this one does NOT create a hole -- it creates a WRONG
              ANSWER.  THE WORDS STOP BEING NAMED CORRECTLY AND
              BECOME NAMED INCORRECTLY: THE UNMODELLED COUNT DOES NOT
              MOVE AT ALL, BECAUSE SOMETHING ELSE STILL NAMES THEM,
              AND THE DISAGREEMENT COUNT RISES BY THE NUMBER THAT
              SOMETHING ELSE GETS WRONG.

  THAT IS THE MIRROR OF POISON 1 AND IT IS WHY BOTH COLUMNS ARE
  PRINTED BESIDE EACH OTHER.  POISON 1 MOVED THE UNMODELLED COLUMN
  AND NOT THE DISAGREEMENT COLUMN; POISON 2 MOVED THE DISAGREEMENT
  COLUMN AND NOT THE UNMODELLED ONE.  A CROSS-CHECK THAT COUNTS
  ONLY AGREEMENTS CALLS BOTH OF THEM A PASS, BECAUSE IN BOTH CASES THE
  INSTRUCTION IS STILL "COMPARED" AND IN BOTH CASES THE COMPARISON
  FOUND NOTHING TO SAY.  A CHECK THAT COUNTS AGREEMENTS AND ONE
  THAT COUNTS HOLES ARE MEASURING DIFFERENT THINGS, AND TWO POISONS
  THAT MOVE ONE COLUMN EACH ARE THE PROOF THAT THEY ARE.  THE
  NAMED COUNT IS A THIRD COLUMN AND IT MOVES IN BOTH POISONS, WHICH IS
  WHY IT IS NOT THE COLUMN THE VERDICT READS: a number that moves
  every time cannot distinguish the two failures.

    BEFORE: 2147 named, 0 disagree, 50 unmodelled
    AFTER : 2147 named, 72 disagree, 50 unmodelled
    DELTA: named +72, disagreements +72, unmodelled +0
    VERDICT: POISON 2 FIRED

    AND THE FIRST DISAGREEMENTS IT PRODUCES, so the failure is
    READABLE rather than only countable:
      0x04b6252f  mine amoadd.w   a0, a1, (a2)        second reader amoadd.w.aq a0, a1, (a2)
      0x02b6252f  mine amoadd.w   a0, a1, (a2)        second reader amoadd.w.rl a0, a1, (a2)
      0x06b6252f  mine amoadd.w   a0, a1, (a2)        second reader amoadd.w.aqrl a0, a1, (a2)
      0x04b6352f  mine amoadd.d   a0, a1, (a2)        second reader amoadd.d.aq a0, a1, (a2)

  POISON 3 -- CLEAR inst[26] and inst[25] in every A-extension word in
              the corpus and ask the decoder whether the mnemonic
              moved.  The number it must move is the count of words
              whose suffix CHANGED.  A FIELD POSITION CLAIMED IN
              PROSE AND NOT MEASURED IS A FIELD POSITION THAT CAN BE
              WRONG WITHOUT ANYTHING LOOKING WRONG -- AND THIS IS THE
              POISON THAT SECTION 4's TABLE DEPENDS ON.

    A-extension words that CARRIED .aq or .rl                  : 28
    whose decoded mnemonic CHANGED when aq/rl were cleared    : 28
    whose decoded mnemonic did NOT change                     : 0
    DELTA: 28 of 28 words moved, 0 did not
    VERDICT: POISON 3 FIRED

  AND THE `still` COLUMN MUST BE ZERO, NOT NON-ZERO.  A poison
  whose victim moves SOME words and not others is a poison that has
  found a partial reader -- which is the class of bug where a decoder
  reads a field correctly in the instruction the author tested and
  not in the eleven others.  EXACT CLAIMS HAVE ONE HONEST
  TREATMENT, AND A COUNT THAT IS "MOSTLY MOVED" IS NOT A COUNT.

  AND THE DENOMINATOR IS THE WORDS THAT CARRIED A SUFFIX, which is a
  DECISION, and it is the first thing to check when a number looks
  wrong.  A COUNT OF INSTANCES THAT COULD NOT HAVE MOVED,
  DIVIDED INTO A TOTAL THAT INCLUDES THEM, IS A NUMBER ABOUT THE
  POISON AND NOT ABOUT THE FIELD.

  POISON 4 -- break the CHECK, not the decoder.  Every instruction this
              file can name is listed, and then an AArch64-shaped
              normaliser -- one that KEEPS the mnemonic and the FIRST
              operand and DELETES the rest -- is applied to both sides
              of a PLANTED set of real disagreements.  The number it
              must move is the number of disagreements that
              DISAPPEAR.

    real disagreements planted (last operand changed): 1933
    of those, the working cross-check CATCHES           : 1933
    of those, the broken rule makes DISAPPEAR           : 1448
    DELTA: 1448 of the 1933 planted disagreements were HIDDEN
    VERDICT: POISON 4 FIRED

FOUR POISONS, FOUR CLAIMED NUMBERS, FOUR NON-ZERO DELTAS:

* poison 1 claims the NAMED and the UNMODELLED counts, and moved them
            by -47 and +47 -- and it claims the DISAGREEMENT count
            stays at +0, which is a claim as load-bearing as either
            of the two that moved
* poison 2 claims the DISAGREEMENT count, and moved it by +72 while
            claiming the UNMODELLED count stays at +0
* poison 3 claims the ORDERING-BIT POSITIONS, and moved 28 of 28 with
            0 unmoved
* poison 4 claims the VISIBILITY of the check, and hid 1448 of 1933

AND THE PAIR WORTH READING TOGETHER IS POISON 1 AND POISON 2,
BECAUSE THEY MOVE DIFFERENT COLUMNS AND EACH CLAIMS THE OTHER'S
COLUMN STAYS PUT.  POISON 1 SAYS A MISSING MODEL MAKES A HOLE;
POISON 2 SAYS A SUPERSEDED MODEL MAKES A MISTAKE.  THE SAME
REMOVAL, IN TWO DIFFERENT PLACES IN THE DISPATCH, PRODUCES A NUMBER
THAT RISES AND A NUMBER THAT DOES NOT -- AND A CROSS-CHECK THAT
PRINTED ONLY THE FIRST WOULD REPORT PROGRESS FOR BOTH.

A poison that cannot move must say so, and this file prints the
string [POISON FAILED] beside the verdict and stops.  A control that did not
run and a control that ran and found nothing are the same number and
two different bugs, and the distinction is made in the return value
rather than in a comment nobody reads.  THE STRING IS NOT ON A
  LINE OF ITS OWN, BECAUSE A READER GREPPING FOR IT WANTS THE VERDICT
  AND NOT THE PARAGRAPH THAT EXPLAINS WHY IT EXISTS.

==========================================================================
SECTION 13 -- EIGHTEEN RETRACTIONS
==========================================================================

  Eighteen of them, and every one was asserted in a draft of this course or in the brief it was written from, measured, and withdrawn. They are printed in full rather than footnoted, and
  `crosscheck.py` asserts their TEXT so that a later edit cannot quietly delete one -- which is the only mechanism that has ever stopped a retraction being quietly dropped.

  Six of the 18 are about a READER or an INSTRUMENT, five are about a COMPARISON, three are about a CLAIM THAT WAS TRUE BUT INCOMPLETE, and four are about the SUBJECT. THE ONES THAT ARE ABOUT THE
  SUBJECT ARE THE ONES WORTH READING TWICE, BECAUSE A LIST THAT IS UNIFORMLY ONE THING IS A LIST NOBODY READ.


  R1  CLAIMED: the inherited decoder names the three vector configuration instructions, because it says they are told apart by inst[31:30]
        SOURCE: this course's own first run of section 11, which reported the inherited decoder naming ten `vsetvli` and nothing else -- and the code it read is the SIBLING's `rvdec.py`, which this course borrows rather than forks
         it computes inst[31:29] -- THREE BITS -- and compares against 0b00, 0b10 and 0b11, so it names `vsetvli` and DECLINES `vsetivli` and `vsetvl`. `vsetvli` is the one instruction whose inst[31:30] and
                inst[31:29] both happen to be 000, and the other two fail the test by a margin of one bit: `vsetivli` at inst[31:29] = 110 gives 6, `vsetvl` at 100 gives 4, and neither is 3 or 2. IT NAMES THE ONE
                INSTRUCTION WHERE TWO BITS AND THREE BITS AGREE AND DECLINES THE TWO WHERE THEY DISAGREE, AND A MODEL THAT IS CORRECT BY COINCIDENCE ON ONE CASE IS A MODEL WHOSE OTHER TWO CASES ARE ALSO COINCIDENCES.
                This is the sibling course's code and the defect is PUBLISHED rather than patched across the course boundary; the fix is a model PREPENDED in this file.

  R2  CLAIMED: the inherited decoder reads the A extension's two ordering bits
        SOURCE: this course's own first cross-check, whose agreement count was too high and whose cause was found by XORing two amo words; again the code is the SIBLING's, and the inherited `a_amo` it borrows is the model that does not read the two bits
         it does not READ them -- it never looks at inst[26] and inst[25] at all, so `amoadd.w`, `amoadd.w.aq`, `amoadd.w.rl` and `amoadd.w.aqrl` all render as THE SAME STRING. The words differ; the output does not.
                A DECODER THAT DOES NOT READ A FIELD RENDERS TWO INSTRUCTIONS IDENTICALLY, AND A CROSS-CHECK THAT COMPARES RENDERINGS WILL CALL THAT AGREEMENT. THIS IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE AArch64 DATA PATH'S
                VACUOUS CROSS-CHECK AND THE OPPOSITE OF ITS SYMPTOM: there the comparison was vacuous and the words were right; here the words are wrong and the comparison is honest. Both are found the same way, by
                asking whether the two sides COULD disagree.

  R3  CLAIMED: the inherited decoder names `fence.tso`
        SOURCE: this course's own first run of section 11 -- and the defect is in the SIBLING's decoder rather than in this file's
         it names it `fence`. `s_fence` reads fm and prints it as a number and gives every word with opcode 0x0f and funct3 = 0 the name `fence`, so fm = 1000 comes back as `fence` with fm = 8 in its field list. A
                DECODER REPORTING AN INSTRUCTION AS THE WRONG INSTRUCTION OF THE SAME FAMILY IS A WORSE FAILURE THAN ONE THAT DECLINES IT, BECAUSE THE WORSE FAILURE STILL PRODUCES A CONFIDENT STRING. And this
                course's own poison 1 would have caught it if the corpus had contained one `fence.tso` -- it did, and the inherited decoder named it.

  R4  CLAIMED: the inherited decoder's `s_misc_mem` names `fence.i` correctly, so nothing needs fixing there
        SOURCE: this course's own first reading of the inherited `s_misc_mem`, which looked correct
         it names it correctly and for the WRONG REASON, and that is worth a paragraph. `fence.i` is funct3 = 1 at the same opcode as the ordering fence, so `s_fence` declines it (it requires funct3 = 0) and
                `s_misc_mem` catches it. The inherited decoder therefore prints the right name and reads fm, pred and succ on it -- and fm, pred and succ are RESERVED in `fence.i`, so all three come back as 0 and a
                reader who does not know that will read them as an ordering statement that nothing needs ordering. RIGHT FOR THE WRONG REASON IS A FAILURE THAT A COMPARISON CANNOT FIND AND ONLY A DOMAIN EXPERT CAN,
                WHICH MAKES IT MORE DANGEROUS THAN AN OUTRIGHT ERROR RATHER THAN LESS.

  R5  CLAIMED: the compile-time comparison shows the compiler emitting `amoswap.w` in a loop instead of a hand-rolled compare-exchange
        SOURCE: the brief this course was written from, which named this as the course's headline measurement
         IT DOES NOT, AND THE CLAIM WAS WRONG in a way that is worth keeping because the wrongness is instructive. THE HAND-ROLLED COMPARE-EXCHANGE IS NOT AN ALTERNATIVE TO BE TRADED AWAY -- IT IS THE ONLY WAY TO
                EXPRESS COMPARE-AND-SWAP, BECAUSE THERE IS NO `cmpxchg` IN THE BASE A EXTENSION AT ALL, AND `amoswap` IS A DIFFERENT OPERATION THAT DOES NOT COMPARE. The compiler emits the ONE INSTRUCTION for the
                ONE-INSTRUCTION OPERATION (`amoswap.w.aqrl` for __atomic_exchange_n) and the LOOP for the loop-requiring one, and there is no trade to make. A COURSE THAT NAMES A COMPILER DECISION WITHOUT CHECKING
                WHETHER THE DECISION EXISTS INCREASES THE GAP BETWEEN WHAT THE COMPILER CAN DO AND WHAT THE COURSE CLAIMS IT DOES.

  R6  CLAIMED: the vectorised loop is measured against the scalar loop at `rv64g` and `rv64gcv`
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 10's table
         that comparison is CONFOUNDED and its numbers were retracted before they were ever printed. `rv64g` and `rv64gcv` differ in TWO letters, and THAT PAIR DIFFERS IN `c` AND `v` AT ONCE -- so every difference in
                the table was a difference between adding a vector unit and removing compression. The clean pair is `rv64gc` against `rv64gcv`, which differ in exactly one letter and keep the `c` extension on both
                sides. THIS IS THE SIBLING ABI COURSE'S OWN FINDING, WHERE THE SAME CONFOUND PRODUCED A -38 THAT WAS THE LIBRARY AND NOT THE ENCODING, AND THE GENERALISATION IS: A -march SWEEP THAT DIFFERS IN MORE
                THAN ONE LETTER IS NOT AN EXPERIMENT ABOUT EITHER.

  R7  CLAIMED: the vector register group is a scalar, so the assembler's refusal of `vl2r.v v1` is a spelling rule
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 9, which described the group as a scalar
         IT IS AN ALIGNMENT CONSTRAINT, and the refusal is the evidence. `vl2r.v v8` assembles and `vl2r.v v1` does not, and the difference is that a group of two must start at an even register. LMUL IS NOT A SCALAR
                AND NOT A WIDER REGISTER -- IT IS A CONSTRAINT ON WHERE A REGISTER GROUP MAY BEGIN, WHICH IS A CONSTRAINT ON A REGISTER ALLOCATOR AND NOT ON A NUMBER. A REFUSAL IS THE CHEAPEST PROOF THAT A CONSTRAINT
                IS REAL AND IT IS ALSO THE PROOF THAT THE CONSTRAINT IS NOT ABOUT SYNTAX.

  R8  CLAIMED: the twelve C11 functions in `fence.c` emit twelve fences
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 7, whose table had a zero in it and no explanation
         they emit fewer than twelve INSTRUCTIONS, and the shortest is a single `ret`. `__atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_RELAXED)` compiles to NO INSTRUCTION, because a relaxed fence asks for nothing and the encoding
                for nothing is no instruction. The first draft of the table reported zero fences for that row as a COUNT and the surrounding prose said the compiler had "not emitted anything visible", which is true
                and is not the interesting half. A RELAXED FENCE IS NOT A NO-OP INSTRUCTION, IT IS THE ABSENCE OF ONE, AND A FUNCTION THAT CONSISTS OF A SINGLE RETURN IS A CORRECT COMPILATION OF A FUNCTION THAT ASKS
                FOR NOTHING. THIS IS ALSO THE FIRST PLACE IN THIS COURSE WHERE A COMPILER REMOVED SOMETHING BY BEING CORRECT, AND IT IS WORTH NAMING BECAUSE THE SIBLING COURSE MEASURED DEAD-CODE ELIMINATION AT -O1
                AND RETRACTED IT FOR CALLING IT A CONVENTION.

  R9  CLAIMED: an acquire load and a sequentially consistent load are the same instruction
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 7, which showed the load column only
         THEY ARE THE SAME INSTRUCTION AND THE SAME FENCE PLUS ANOTHER FENCE, so the first version of the table showed one `lw` for each and read the difference off the load alone. MEASURED: `load_acquire` is `lw`
                with one `fence r, rw` after it, and `load_seqcst` is `fence rw, rw`, `lw`, `fence r, rw` -- TWO fences for ONE load. THE EXTRA CONSTRAINT A SEQ_CST LOAD CARRIES IS ON ITS RELATION TO OTHER
                OPERATIONS, SO IT IS DISCHARGED BY ORDERING THE NEIGHBOURS AND NOT BY CHANGING THE LOAD. IT IS STILL TRUE THAT THE LOADS ARE IDENTICAL, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THE CLAIM WAS TRUE AND INCOMPLETE,
                WHICH IS A DISTINCTION WORTH NAMING: A CLAIM THAT IS TRUE AND HAS A QUALIFIER HIDING IN THE NEXT COLUMN IS A CLAIM THAT HAS BEEN HALF-WRITTEN.

  R10  CLAIMED: this course can say what an atomic instruction DOES
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 5
         IT IS NOT MEASURED, and the file says so in its header, in section 1, at the end of every affected section, and in the table in section 15 rather than in a disclaimer. No reservation is ever held, no AMO is
                ever observed to be atomic, no fence is ever observed to order anything, and no vector instruction is ever executed. AN ATOMIC INSTRUCTION IS A PROMISE ABOUT OBSERVABLE BEHAVIOUR, AND EVERY
                MEASUREMENT AVAILABLE ON THIS HOST IS A MEASUREMENT ABOUT THE PROMISE'S TEXT: THE BIT THAT CARRIES IT, THE PAIR THAT IMPLEMENTS IT, AND THE COMPILER'S CHOICE ABOUT WHETHER TO EMIT IT AT ALL. WHAT IS
                MEASURED IS THAT CLANG EMITS `lr.w.aqrl` AND `sc.w.rl` WITH A BRANCH BETWEEN THEM; WHAT IS NOT MEASURED IS THAT ANY OF IT WORKS.

  R11  CLAIMED: the ordering model is a difference of degree from x86-64's and AArch64's, because all three are memory models with barriers
        SOURCE: `docs/course-mission.md`, which says "differs in kind" and supplies no mechanism
         it is a difference of FORM and the section says so. x86-64 describes a BASELINE that barriers can override; AArch64 gives each ACCESS a MODE, so `ldar` and `ldr` are different opcodes; RISC-V defines the
                model AS A RELATION BETWEEN TWO NAMED SETS in one instruction. A BASELINE AND AN ESCAPE HATCH IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU DO NOTHING; AN ACCESS MODE IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT EACH INSTRUCTION
                MEANS; A PREDECESSOR/SUCCESSOR PAIR IS A CLAIM ABOUT A RELATION BETWEEN TWO SETS. THE THIRD IS THE ONLY ONE IN WHICH THE FENCE IS THE PRIMITIVE RATHER THAN THE EXCEPTION, AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT KIND
                OF DIFFERENCE AND NOT A DIFFERENT DEGREE OF THE SAME ONE.

  R12  CLAIMED: the twelve named `fence.tso` words in the corpus prove the compiler emits it
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 7, which counted the corpus
         they prove the CORPUS contains it, which is not the same claim, and the first version of section 7 counted corpus occurrences and called the total a compiler statistic. The two were separated by assembling
                ONE fence line at a time and asking which word came back: the compiler emits `fence.tso` for exactly one of the six C11 fence strengths, `__ATOMIC_ACQ_REL`, and `fence rw, rw` for `__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST`.
                A NUMBER ABOUT WHAT WAS FED TO IT IS NOT A NUMBER ABOUT WHAT WAS CHOSEN, AND THE TWO ARE THE SAME FAILURE THIS SECTION'S CORPUS-COMPLETENESS CHECK GUARDS AGAINST.

  R13  CLAIMED: `fence.tso` is a weaker barrier than `fence rw, rw` on every implementation, so emitting it is a request
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 7, which had the direction of the superset backwards
         the manual says something STRONGER and more interesting than that, and the first version had it backwards in a way that would have been a good saying and a false one. THE MANUAL SAYS THAT an implementation
                may ignore `fm`: "Because FENCE RW,RW IMPOSES A SUPERSET OF THE ORDERINGS THAT FENCE.TSO IMPOSES, IT IS CORRECT TO IGNORE THE fm FIELD AND IMPLEMENT FENCE.TSO AS FENCE RW,RW." SO FENCE.TSO IS A
                REQUEST FOR A WEAKER BARRIER THAN THE ONE IT IS SPELLED LIKE, AND A BASE IMPLEMENTATION IS EXPLICITLY PERMITTED TO GIVE THE STRONGER ONE -- WHICH MEANS "fence.tso" IS NOT PORTABLE ADVICE AND ALSO NOT
                A WEAKER BARRIER, AND THE ONLY HONEST THING TO SAY IS BOTH.

  R14  CLAIMED: the vector extension's tail policy is a performance detail
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 8, which called the policy a performance detail
         IT IS A LIBRARY CONTRACT MADE OF BITS, and the specification says the outcome is not even deterministic. "EACH DESTINATION ELEMENT CAN BE EITHER LEFT UNDISTURBED OR OVERWRITTEN WITH 1S, IN ANY COMBINATION,
                AND THE PATTERN OF UNDISTURBED OR OVERWRITTEN WITH 1S IS NOT REQUIRED TO BE DETERMINISTIC WHEN THE INSTRUCTION IS EXECUTED WITH THE SAME INPUTS." SO THE FAST POLICY IS NOT THE PORTABLE ONE, AND THE
                COMPILER PICKS THE FAST ONE BY DEFAULT, AND THE ONLY WAY A READER LEARNS THAT IS BY DECODING TWO BITS. THE PORTABILITY PROBLEM IN THE VECTOR EXTENSION IS NOT THE INSTRUCTION SET -- IT IS TWO BITS THE
                SPECIFICATION DELIBERATELY LEAVES UNDETERMINED.

  R15  CLAIMED: a `vsetvli` sweep of 112 variants shows eleven bits in use
        SOURCE: this course's own first sweep, which counted bits SET in any variant rather than bits that MOVE
         it shows EIGHT, because zimm[10:8] is constant zero in every reachable variant -- three bits of an eleven-bit field that no `vsetvli` the assembler will emit ever uses. THE RESERVED BITS ARE NOT WASTED, AND
                THE INHERITED DECODER'S OWN COMMENT ABOUT THE ELEMENT WIDTH IS A LIVING EXAMPLE: IT SAID "THREE BITS OF THE FIELD" WITH FOUR CORRECT zimm STRINGS AND NEVER SAID WHICH THREE. A FIELD MAP MEASURED BY
                SWEEP RECORDS WHICH BITS MOVE, AND THREE BITS THAT NEVER MOVE LOOK EXACTLY LIKE THREE BITS THAT DO. A COMMENT THAT GIVES THE WIDTH OF A FIELD WITHOUT GIVING ITS POSITION IS A COMMENT THAT CANNOT BE
                CHECKED AGAINST THE FIELD.

  R16  CLAIMED: the corpus's `fence` rows all have `rs1 = rd = 0` because the assembler emits zeroes there
        SOURCE: this course's own first reading of the fence field table
         THEY HAVE ZEROES BECAUSE THE MANUAL REQUIRES THEM TO: "The unused fields in the FENCE instructions -- rs1 and rd -- are reserved for finer-grain fences in future extensions. For forward compatibility, base
                implementations shall ignore these fields, and standard software shall ZERO these fields." TWO RESERVED FIELDS IN AN INSTRUCTION WHOSE OTHER TWO FIELDS CARRY THE WHOLE MODEL IS THE TELL THAT THE
                ENCODING WAS DESIGNED FOR A FUTURE NOBODY HAS BUILT YET. The first draft said the zeroes were a property of the assembler, which is true and is not why.

  R17  CLAIMED: the two readers agreeing means this decoder is right
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 11, which said "the two readers agree"
         it means THIS decoder and one other piece of software from the SAME LLVM TREE agree. `clang` assembled the corpus and `llvm-objdump-21` disassembled it, and GNU binutils has no RISC-V target on this host, so
                THE TWO READERS SHARE AN ASSEMBLER, AND A CROSS-CHECK BETWEEN ONE TOOLCHAIN'S OUTPUT AND ITS OWN VIEW OF THAT OUTPUT CANNOT FIND A BUG IN THE SHARED ASSUMPTIONS. THE CORRECTION IS NOT "DO NOT TRUST
                THE CROSS-CHECK" BUT "SAY WHAT IT PROVES": this file and one other piece of software agree about what the bytes mean, and NOT that either agrees with silicon. AND THAT SENTENCE IS LIMIT 11, IN THE
                ARTIFACT'S OWN WORDS, BECAUSE A LIMIT PRINTED ONCE AT THE END IS A LIMIT THAT GETS SKIMMED PAST.

  R18  CLAIMED: the lock-prefix count on RISC-V is zero because the assembler refuses `lock`
        SOURCE: this course's own first draft of section 2, which printed the zero without the other count
         the assembler refusing `lock` is a fact about the ASSEMBLER, and the claim needs to be about the ARCHITECTURE. The measurement that carries the claim is the OTHER count: zero instructions in the corpus need
                a prefix to become atomic, against nine operations plus a reservation pair at two widths and four orderings that are atomic with no prefix at all. "ZERO" IS AN ABSENCE OF FEATURES IF YOU PRINT IT
                ALONE AND A DESIGN DECISION IF YOU PRINT IT BESIDE THE COUNT IT REPLACED. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN x86-64 AND RISC-V IS NOT THAT ONE HAS EIGHTEEN AND THE OTHER NONE -- IT IS THAT x86-64 MADE ATOMICITY A
                PROPERTY OF AN EXISTING INSTRUCTION AND RISC-V MADE IT A CATEGORY OF INSTRUCTION.

THE CLAIM THAT TIES THE LIST TOGETHER is at the end of it:

  NOTHING HERE IS A MISTAKE ABOUT HOW A COMPUTER WORKS.  That is
  EIGHTEEN COURSES IN A ROW, and it is the most interesting thing
  about the list.  The RISC-V atomics, fence and vector extension did
  not surprise this course once.  What surprised it was a two-bit
  field two of whose four values are invalid on half the
  instructions that carry it, a decoder that computed three bits
  where the encoding has two, a bit that is "release" in one opcode
  and "mask" in another, a policy the specification declines to make
  deterministic, and a tail policy the compiler picks the fast way by
  default.

EIGHTEEN COURSES IN A ROW, AND EVERY SINGLE RETRACTION IS A
DISCOVERY ABOUT THE MACHINERY BUILT TO READ THE HARDWARE.

==========================================================================
SECTION 14 -- THE MEASURED/QUOTED BOUNDARY, THE LIMITS, AND WHERE EVERY CLAIM CAME FROM
==========================================================================

  Everything in the last thirteen sections carries one of three labels, and on most pages you would be forgiven for not noticing. This section prints all of them, in one table, with the count -- and
  the COUNT is the finding, because this is a course about ENCODINGS and about a COMPILER'S CHOICES, and those are exactly the two things a host without hardware can measure.

  THE RATIO IS NOT A QUALITY SCORE. It is a map of the subject. The neutral courses `simd` and `smp` and the per-architecture courses `x86simd` and `a64simd` all had hardware, so their quoted thirds
  are smaller for the same reason: what a document says is not measurable on a host with no machine, and what IS measurable -- a bit position, a field map, an instruction count, a refusal -- is the
  part a compiler author needs and the part a page-table writer needs.


